(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1b.791bf7d59d.js"]={"7":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the tonicity of the initial saliva secreted by acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"Hypotonic to plasma","B":"Hypertonic to plasma","C":"Varies with the diet","D":"Twice plasma osmolarity","E":"Isotonic to plasma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete an initial saliva that is isotonic, with Na+, K+, Cl−, and HCO3− concentrations similar to plasma. Saliva becomes hypotonic later, in the ducts. It does not become hypertonic or reach twice plasma osmolarity, and diet does not set its tonicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, acinar cell secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous salivary secretions contain alpha amylase and lipase (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands). COMLEX: the parotid gland is primarily serous.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of salivary ductal cells allows the final saliva to become hypotonic?","choices":{"A":"Active water secretion","B":"Potassium reabsorption","C":"Rapid acinar flow","D":"Low water permeability","E":"Bicarbonate absorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ductal cells absorb more NaCl than they secrete KHCO3, and because they are relatively impermeable to water, water does not follow the absorbed solute, so the saliva becomes hypotonic. Ducts do not secrete water. They secrete rather than reabsorb potassium and bicarbonate. Rapid flow makes saliva closer to isotonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: final saliva is hypotonic with lower Na+ and Cl− and higher K+ and HCO3− than plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular gland makes most of the saliva in the mouth (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"What does contraction of salivary myoepithelial cells accomplish?","choices":{"A":"Secretes salivary amylase","B":"Expels stored saliva","C":"Reabsorbs ductal sodium","D":"Dilates gland arterioles","E":"Lowers saliva tonicity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Myoepithelial (basket) cells lie between the basal membranes of acinar and ductal cells and the basal lamina; on neural stimulation they contract, compressing the acini and shortening the ducts to eject saliva. Acinar cells secrete amylase. Ductal cells reabsorb sodium and lower tonicity. Bradykinin from kallikrein dilates the arterioles. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, myoepithelial cell function); Costanzo Ch. 8 (structure of the salivary glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic stimulation contracts myoepithelial cells (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: the sublingual gland is primarily mucous (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which second messenger pathway mediates parasympathetic stimulation of salivary acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and Ca2+","B":"Cyclic AMP","C":"Cyclic GMP","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors, which act through IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ to produce a large volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva. Cyclic AMP is the sympathetic beta receptor messenger. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and tyrosine kinase are not the salivary parasympathetic pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parasympathetic input to the salivary glands travels in CN VII and CN IX (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs cause dry mouth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands synapse?","choices":{"A":"Submandibular ganglion","B":"Pterygopalatine ganglion","C":"Inferior cervical ganglion","D":"Geniculate ganglion","E":"Superior cervical ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers arise from thoracic segments T1 to T3 and synapse in the superior cervical ganglion; postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto the glands. The submandibular ganglion is a parasympathetic relay for the submandibular gland. The pterygopalatine, inferior cervical, and geniculate ganglia are not the salivary sympathetic relay in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: both parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation increase saliva, and parasympathetic control is dominant (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is regulated by autonomic nerves rather than GI hormones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor decreases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands?","choices":{"A":"Oral tactile input","B":"Dehydration","C":"Sour taste","D":"Smell of food","E":"Conditioned reflexes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands is decreased by fear, sleep, and dehydration. It is increased by food, taste (especially sour stimuli), smell, tactile oral stimuli, nausea, and conditioned reflexes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (factors that modulate parasympathetic input).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary stasis from dehydration is associated with salivary duct stones (First Aid 2025 GI, sialolithiasis). COMLEX: Pavlov conditioned reflexes increase salivation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme in the parietal cell cytoplasm generates the H+ that is secreted into the gastric lumen?","choices":{"A":"Adenylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase A","D":"Carbonic anhydrase","E":"Histidine decarboxylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase combines CO2 and H2O into H2CO3, which dissociates into H+ for secretion and HCO3− for absorption into the blood. Adenylyl cyclase, phospholipase C, and protein kinase A are signaling enzymes that regulate the pump. Histidine decarboxylase makes histamine. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, intracellular generation of H+ and HCO3−); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid and intrinsic factor (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal secretory products). COMLEX: gastric pH is normally between 1 and 2 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Through which apical pathway does chloride enter the gastric lumen during HCl secretion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","B":"Sodium chloride cotransporter","C":"Chloride ATPase pump","D":"Potassium chloride symport","E":"Passive chloride channels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cl− diffuses passively into the canalicular lumen through apical Cl− channels and combines with secreted H+ to form HCl. The chloride bicarbonate exchanger sits on the basolateral membrane and brings Cl− into the cell. Sodium chloride and potassium chloride cotransport and a chloride ATPase are not part of the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell apical membrane contains the H+/K+ ATPase and Cl− channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vomiting gastric acid causes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the bicarbonate generated in parietal cells go during acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Into the gastric lumen","B":"Into the canaliculus","C":"Into gastric venous blood","D":"Into parietal cell vesicles","E":"Into the mucus gel layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HCO3− made by carbonic anhydrase leaves across the basolateral membrane into the blood, producing the alkaline tide. It is not secreted into the lumen or canaliculus, where H+ goes. It is not stored in vesicles. Bicarbonate trapped in surface mucus comes from surface epithelial cells, not parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport and the alkaline tide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 3).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the alkaline tide raises blood pH after gastric acid secretion, such as after meals or vomiting (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: absorbed HCO3− is later secreted back into the gut in pancreatic juice (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what explains potentiation among the gastric acid secretagogues?","choices":{"A":"Different second messengers","B":"Shared receptor binding","C":"Common G protein coupling","D":"Competitive receptor antagonism","E":"Increased gastrin clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine acts through cAMP while ACh and gastrin act through IP3/Ca2+, and ACh and gastrin also release histamine from ECL cells, so combined stimuli give more acid than the sum of their separate effects. Each agent has its own receptor, so they do not share binding or one G protein. Antagonism and gastrin clearance reduce rather than amplify the response. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, potentiation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase in parietal cells supplies the H+ that the apical pump secretes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: combined secretagogues produce more acid than expected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, gastrin from antral G cells binds its receptors on which two cell types in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Chief and ECL cells","B":"Parietal and D cells","C":"Chief and mucous cells","D":"Parietal and ECL cells","E":"Mucous and D cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin binds its receptors on both parietal cells and ECL cells, so it can stimulate parietal cells directly and also act through ECL cells. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and are not the gastrin targets named in the lecture. D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits acid. Mucous cells secrete mucus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrin acts on parietal cells and ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released from G cells of the gastric antrum (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: PPIs are used to treat Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, proton pump inhibitors).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which receptor mechanism does somatostatin use directly on parietal cells to reduce acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Gq protein and IP3","B":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","C":"Inhibitory G protein","D":"Ligand gated channel","E":"Nuclear receptor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Somatostatin binds Gi coupled receptors on parietal cells, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and lowering cAMP, which antagonizes histamine. Gq and IP3 are the ACh and gastrin pathway. Tyrosine kinase receptors, ligand gated channels, and nuclear receptors are not the somatostatin mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D cells release somatostatin, which acts through a Gi cascade to reduce H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: lower cAMP reduces protein kinase A activity in the parietal cell (lecture SDL 7).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of meal-induced acid secretion occurs in the gastric phase?","choices":{"A":"About 60 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 10 percent","D":"About 90 percent","E":"About 45 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The gastric phase accounts for about 60% of the acid response, the cephalic phase about 30%, and the intestinal phase about 10%. Ninety and 45 percent do not match any phase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, phases of gastric acid secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (phases of HCl secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase is triggered by distension and protein digestion products (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the intestinal phase accounts for about 10 percent of acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gastric mucosal defense rapidly seals small erosions?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin secretion","B":"Mucosal fibrosis","C":"Cell restitution","D":"Parietal hyperplasia","E":"Gastrin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid epithelial cell restitution seals small erosions, alongside mucus and trapped HCO3−, tight junctions, and mucosal blood flow. Pepsin is a damaging factor. Fibrosis is scarring rather than a rapid defense. Parietal cell hyperplasia and gastrin increase acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and give physical protection from acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: peptic ulcers reflect an imbalance of protective and damaging factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which H. pylori enzyme lets the organism survive in gastric acid?","choices":{"A":"Catalase","B":"Urease","C":"Lipase","D":"Protease","E":"Mucinase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"H. pylori urease converts urea into ammonia and CO2, forming an alkaline ammonia cloud that protects it from luminal acid. Catalase, lipase, protease, and mucinase are not the lecture mechanism for acid survival. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (urease hydrolyzes urea to form ammonia, which neutralizes gastric acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the urea breath test detects H. pylori urease activity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: H. pylori is treated with triple or quadruple therapy that includes a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman is dehydrated, and her salivary flow falls below 1 mL/min. Compared with her saliva at a high flow rate, which change in her final saliva is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sodium concentration","B":"Higher chloride concentration","C":"Higher bicarbonate concentration","D":"Higher potassium concentration","E":"Isotonic osmolarity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low flow rates, saliva spends more time in contact with ductal cells, so more Na+ and Cl− are reabsorbed and more K+ is secreted; final saliva is most hypotonic with the highest K+. Sodium and chloride are lowest at low flow. Bicarbonate is lowest at low flow because its secretion is selectively stimulated during active salivation. Isotonic saliva is seen at high flow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (effect of flow rate on composition of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at the highest flow rates saliva most closely resembles plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: dehydration reduces parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy chews sour candy, and his salivary flow rises to about 4 mL/min. His salivary bicarbonate is higher than at rest even though the saliva spends less time in the ducts. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"Ductal bicarbonate reabsorption","B":"Higher plasma bicarbonate","C":"Water reabsorption by ducts","D":"Slower acinar fluid secretion","E":"Stimulated ductal secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCO3− is the exception to the contact time rule: its ductal secretion is selectively stimulated when saliva production is stimulated, so its concentration rises with flow rate. Ducts secrete rather than reabsorb bicarbonate. Plasma bicarbonate does not change with a sour stimulus. Ductal cells are relatively impermeable to water. Acinar secretion speeds up, not slows, during stimulation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (HCO3− is not described by the contact time explanation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sour taste increases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary bicarbonate buffers ingested food.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman in a physiology study has saliva collected at a low flow rate, and its Na+ concentration is far below that of plasma. Through which luminal membrane transporter do her ductal cells take up this Na+?","choices":{"A":"Sodium potassium pumps","B":"Sodium glucose carriers","C":"Epithelial sodium channels","D":"Sodium hydrogen exchangers","E":"Sodium phosphate carriers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The luminal membrane of salivary ductal cells contains epithelial Na+ channels (ENaC), CFTR Cl− channels, Cl−/HCO3− exchange, and H+/K+ exchange; Na+ enters through ENaC and is pumped out across the basolateral membrane. The Na+/K+ ATPase sits on the basolateral membrane, not the luminal one. Sodium glucose, sodium hydrogen, and sodium phosphate carriers are not the ductal luminal transporters listed. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2: ductal luminal and basolateral transporters); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR is the luminal Cl− channel of salivary ductal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is not a simple ultrafiltrate of plasma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old man starts an anticholinergic drug for bladder spasm and now has a dry mouth during meals. Blockade of which receptors on his salivary cells best explains the loss of watery saliva?","choices":{"A":"Beta adrenergic receptors","B":"Alpha adrenergic receptors","C":"Histamine H2 receptors","D":"Muscarinic M3 receptors","E":"Dopamine D2 receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors on acinar and ductal cells, and the IP3/Ca2+ cascade produces a high volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva; blocking M3 removes this dominant drive. Beta and alpha adrenergic receptors mediate the smaller sympathetic response. H2 and D2 receptors are not the salivary parasympathetic receptors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: atropine blocks muscarinic receptors (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: parasympathetic control of saliva is dominant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man gives a stressful speech and notices that his saliva becomes thick and scant. Activation of which pathway in his salivary cells produces this protein rich secretion?","choices":{"A":"M3 receptors raising IP3","B":"Nicotinic receptors on acini","C":"H2 receptors raising cAMP","D":"Gi receptors lowering cAMP","E":"Beta receptors raising cAMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion release norepinephrine onto beta adrenergic receptors, which activate adenylyl cyclase and raise cAMP, producing a smaller volume of viscous, protein and mucin rich saliva. M3 and IP3 mediate the watery parasympathetic response. Nicotinic receptors are on ganglion cells, not acini. H2 receptors are on parietal cells. A Gi pathway would lower cAMP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from T1 to T3 (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: sympathetic alpha receptors are also present on acinar cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man in a study has parasympathetic stimulation of his submandibular gland, and gland blood flow rises sharply along with secretion. Which acinar product mediates this vasodilation?","choices":{"A":"Alpha amylase","B":"Lingual lipase","C":"Kallikrein","D":"Mucin glycoprotein","E":"Secretory IgA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During high salivary activity, acinar cells secrete kallikrein, which cleaves high molecular weight kininogen into bradykinin, a potent local vasodilator that accounts for the high salivary blood flow. Alpha amylase and lingual lipase begin carbohydrate and lipid digestion. Mucin lubricates food. Secretory IgA is an immune product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (organic constituents of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: maximal salivary blood flow is more than 10 times that of exercising muscle when corrected for size (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular duct is Wharton duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has parietal cells studied during acid secretion. As each HCO3− ion leaves across the basolateral membrane, which ion enters the cell in exchange?","choices":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Chloride","C":"Potassium","D":"Hydrogen","E":"Phosphate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The basolateral membrane of the parietal cell carries a Cl−/HCO3− exchanger, so HCO3− leaves for the blood as Cl− enters; that Cl− then diffuses into the lumen through apical Cl− channels to accompany the secreted H+. Na+ and K+ are moved by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase, not by this exchanger. H+ is pumped into the lumen. Phosphate is not part of HCl secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion: basolateral Cl−/HCO3− exchangers, apical Cl− channels); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport). The direction of Cl− entry is inferred from the exchanger swapping Cl− for HCO3−.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell basolateral membrane also contains the Na+/K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: net HCl secretion is paired with net HCO3− absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has gastric glands studied after surgery. Blocking the apical K+ channels of her parietal cells lowers acid output even though the pump is intact. Why?","choices":{"A":"Carbonic anhydrase is inhibited","B":"Chloride channels close","C":"Pump loses its exchange ion","D":"Histamine release falls","E":"Bicarbonate exits apically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The H+/K+ ATPase secretes H+ in exchange for luminal K+, and K+ recycles back into the canaliculus through apical K+ channels; without that recycling, little K+ is available for exchange and H+ secretion falls. Carbonic anhydrase, chloride channels, and histamine release are not directly affected. Bicarbonate exits across the basolateral membrane. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+/K+ ATPase is a primary active transporter that moves H+ and K+ against their gradients (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors block this pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man takes omeprazole for a gastric ulcer, and his acid output falls in response to every stimulus, including histamine and gastrin. Which step does the drug block?","choices":{"A":"Apical proton pump","B":"Parietal H2 receptors","C":"Parietal M3 receptors","D":"CCK2 receptors","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole irreversibly block the apical H+/K+ ATPase, the final step of acid secretion shared by every secretagogue, which suppresses both meal stimulated and nocturnal acid secretion. H2, M3, and CCK2 receptor blockade would each remove one stimulus. Carbonic anhydrase is not the drug target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs can cause malabsorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman takes cimetidine, and her acid response to vagal stimulation and to gastrin falls much more than expected from blocking one secretagogue. Which mechanism explains this larger effect?","choices":{"A":"Direct M3 receptor blockade","B":"Lost histamine potentiation","C":"CCK2 receptor blockade","D":"Proton pump inhibition","E":"Gastrin release inhibition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Histamine potentiates the actions of ACh and gastrin, so an H2 blocker removes both the direct histamine effect and the histamine-potentiated effects of ACh and gastrin. Cimetidine does not block M3 or CCK2 receptors, inhibit the proton pump, or stop gastrin release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation and H2 receptor blocking agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine inhibits cytochrome P450 and has antiandrogenic effects (First Aid 2025 GI, H2 blockers). COMLEX: famotidine and nizatidine are other H2 antagonists.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has gastric pH recorded through a meal. The pH stays relatively high while food is in his stomach but drops sharply once the meal empties into the duodenum. What explains the drop?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin secretion rises","B":"Somatostatin release falls","C":"Vagal tone increases","D":"Pepsin activity increases","E":"Less food buffer remains"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Food buffers secreted H+, so gastric contents are less acidic while the meal is present; once chyme moves into the small intestine, buffering falls and continued H+ secretion lowers pH, which then inhibits gastrin and triggers somatostatin release to slow acid secretion. Gastrin falls and somatostatin rises as pH drops. Vagal tone falls rather than rises as the meal leaves, and pepsin activity does not lower pH. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distension of the stomach drives gastric phase acid secretion through vagovagal and local reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the cephalic phase is mediated by the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman in a study has her vagus nerves stimulated, and ACh binds muscarinic receptors on her parietal cells. Which enzyme does this receptor activate to drive acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase C","B":"Adenylyl cyclase","C":"Guanylyl cyclase","D":"Tyrosine kinase","E":"Lipoxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M3 receptors activate phospholipase C, which liberates diacylglycerol and IP3; IP3 releases intracellular Ca2+, and Ca2+ and diacylglycerol activate protein kinases that drive H+ secretion. Adenylyl cyclase is the histamine H2 pathway. Guanylyl cyclase, tyrosine kinase, and lipoxygenase are not the parietal M3 pathway. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (substances that alter HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACh acts on M3 receptors through a Gq cascade to increase H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: atropine blocks direct vagal stimulation of parietal cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 85-year-old man has H. pylori infection and a duodenal ulcer. The infection also spreads to the duodenum. Which duodenal defense does it impair?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic enzyme output","B":"Bile salt secretion","C":"Duodenal villus height","D":"Local bicarbonate output","E":"Duodenal mucosal IgA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spread of H. pylori to the duodenum inhibits duodenal HCO3− secretion, so acid delivered from the stomach is poorly neutralized and erodes the mucosa. Pancreatic enzymes, bile salts, villus height, and mucosal IgA are not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (duodenal ulcers); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are caused by H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: H. pylori is diagnosed by urea breath test, fecal antigen, or biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman has an H. pylori gastric ulcer. Her measured net acid secretion is lower than normal, and her serum gastrin is raised. What explains the low net acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","B":"Back leak into the mucosa","C":"Gastrin receptor loss","D":"Excess somatostatin release","E":"Vagal nerve injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gastric ulcers the mucosal barrier is defective, so some secreted H+ leaks back into the damaged mucosa and net H+ secretion is lower than normal; the reduced net acid removes feedback on G cells and raises gastrin. Autoimmunity against parietal cells causes atrophic gastritis. Gastrin receptor loss, excess somatostatin, and vagal injury are not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric ulcers and Table 8.5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori releases CagA toxin after attaching to epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic NSAID use is a cause of gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman in a study smells and chews food and then spits it out, and her acid output rises before food reaches her stomach. Which vagal actions drive this response?","choices":{"A":"ACh and GRP release","B":"Gastrin and CCK release","C":"Secretin and GIP release","D":"Motilin and GIP release","E":"Histamine and somatostatin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, vagal efferents release ACh onto parietal cells and GRP onto antral G cells, so acid rises directly and through gastrin. Gastrin is released secondarily via GRP rather than by the vagus itself, and CCK is not part of the cephalic phase. Secretin, GIP, and motilin are not vagal transmitters. Histamine comes from ECL cells, and somatostatin inhibits acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, phases of gastric acid secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cephalic phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic phase accounts for about 30% of meal stimulated acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: smell, taste, chewing, and conditioned reflexes start the cephalic phase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy chews bread until it tastes sweet, but digestion of the starch by his salivary enzyme stops soon after he swallows. Why?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin degrades the starch","B":"Gastric acid inactivates it","C":"Intrinsic factor binds it","D":"Pancreatic lipase blocks it","E":"Bile salts inhibit it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete alpha amylase, which begins starch digestion in the mouth, but salivary amylase is inactivated by the low pH of the gastric contents, so it plays little role overall. Pepsin digests protein, not starch. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12. Pancreatic lipase and bile salts act in the duodenum on fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, acinar cell secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (digestion of carbohydrates: salivary amylase is inactivated by low gastric pH).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic amylase completes starch digestion in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lingual lipase begins the digestion of lipids in the mouth (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy is badly frightened before a school play, and his sympathetic activity is high. His mouth is dry even though sympathetic input stimulates his acinar cells. Which sympathetic effect also limits his fluid output?","choices":{"A":"Beta receptor blockade","B":"Ductal water absorption","C":"Reduced gland blood flow","D":"Myoepithelial relaxation","E":"Acinar cell apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic stimulation raises cAMP and produces a small volume of viscous saliva, but concurrent sympathetic vasoconstriction reduces local blood supply and blunts overall fluid output; fear also lowers parasympathetic drive. Norepinephrine activates rather than blocks beta receptors. Ductal water absorption is not a sympathetic effect. Stimulation contracts rather than relaxes myoepithelial cells. Fear does not kill acinar cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway and parasympathetic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from spinal segments T1 to T3 (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary glands have a very high blood flow that rises when saliva production is stimulated (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl in a study receives an infusion of CCK, and her parietal cells secrete acid much as they do in response to gastrin. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"Both bind CCK2 receptors","B":"CCK triggers vagal ACh","C":"CCK releases antral gastrin","D":"Both bind H2 receptors","E":"CCK inhibits D cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gastrin acts on parietal cells through CCK2 receptors, which have equal affinity for gastrin and CCK, whereas CCK1 receptors are specific for CCK; CCK can therefore act at the same receptor. CCK does not act by releasing vagal ACh or antral gastrin. H2 receptors bind histamine. Inhibiting D cells is not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrin binds CCK2 receptors on parietal cells); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin acts through a Gq cascade to increase H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: CCK1 receptors are specific for CCK (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"An 86-year-old man in a study receives somatostatin, and his acid output falls more than its direct parietal cell effect predicts. Which paracrine action adds to the fall?","choices":{"A":"More vagal ACh release","B":"Blocking of H2 receptors","C":"Inhibited carbonic anhydrase","D":"Less ECL histamine release","E":"Closed apical chloride channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Besides its direct action on parietal cells, somatostatin acts by a paracrine mechanism to inhibit histamine release from ECL cells, reducing the stimulatory action of histamine. It does not raise vagal ACh, block H2 receptors, inhibit carbonic anhydrase, or close chloride channels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion, indirect pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the somatostatin analog octreotide is used to manage carcinoid tumors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor management). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits acid secretion by both direct and indirect pathways (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl has healthy gastric mucosa. Some pepsin from her gastric juice diffuses into the surface mucus layer. Why does it not digest her epithelium?","choices":{"A":"High pH inactivates it","B":"Mucus digests the pepsin","C":"Tight junctions bind it","D":"Prostaglandins degrade it","E":"Blood flow carries it off"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surface cells secrete mucus that traps HCO3−, creating a relatively alkaline layer at the cell surface; pepsin that penetrates this layer is inactivated in the high HCO3− environment. Mucus does not digest pepsin. Tight junctions limit back-diffusion of acid rather than bind pepsin. Prostaglandins do not degrade pepsin. Mucosal blood flow removes back-diffusing H+, not pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric diffusion barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the alkaline mucus gel neutralizes acid and inactivates pepsin to prevent autodigestion (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: H+ and pepsin together erode the mucosa in peptic ulcer disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman has a duodenal ulcer, and imaging shows no pancreatic or duodenal tumor. Serum gastrin is measured while fasting and again after a test meal. Which pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low fasting and normal after meals","B":"Low fasting and low after meals","C":"High fasting and normal after meals","D":"Normal fasting and low after meals","E":"Normal fasting and high after meals"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In persons with duodenal ulcers, baseline gastrin may be normal, but gastrin secretion in response to a meal is increased. Low fasting and low or normal meal values do not fit duodenal ulcer disease. A high fasting value with a normal meal response, or a normal fasting value with a low meal response, is not the described pattern. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (duodenal ulcers: normal baseline gastrin, increased meal response; Zollinger-Ellison syndrome). The fasting and meal values in the wrong rows are not described by the sources.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcer pain improves with meals, whereas gastric ulcer pain follows meals (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: a gastrinoma can sit in the pancreas or duodenum within the gastrinoma triangle (lecture SDL 7).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl07_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":4,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has taken a nonprescription anti-inflammatory pill at high dose every day for several months and now has burning epigastric pain. Endoscopy shows multiple gastric erosions. Which of the following sets of gastric mucosal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Acid secretion","Bicarbonate secretion","Mucosal blood flow"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower PGE2, so the pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is correct. PGE2 normally acts through Gi receptors on parietal cells to oppose histamine, stimulates mucus and HCO3− secretion, and maintains mucosal blood flow; losing it tips the balance toward damaging factors. In the barrier-loss view, which Robbins emphasizes, prostaglandins support nearly every mucosal defense (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution), so the injury comes mainly from weakened protection rather than from extra acid; Robbins notes that prostaglandins also reduce acid secretion, so their loss fits the ↑ acid that Bootcamp gives. The pattern (↓ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is an H2 receptor antagonist such as famotidine, which reversibly blocks parietal cell H2 receptors and lowers acid secretion. The pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is a Cushing ulcer, in which brain injury and raised intracranial pressure increase vagal stimulation of parietal cells. The pattern (↔ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is a Curling ulcer, in which burns or trauma cause hypovolemia and reduced gastric blood flow. The pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is antral H. pylori infection in duodenal ulcer disease, in which loss of D cell somatostatin raises gastrin and acid delivery while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, lines 1368–1370, prostaglandins bind Gi receptors on parietal cells; section 5, PGE2 preserves the mucosal barrier; SDL 9, section 2, lines 1715–1719, antral H. pylori in duodenal ulcer disease; SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonists); Costanzo Ch. 8 (PGE2 stimulates HCO3− and mucus secretion and increases mucosal blood flow); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (line 1044, NSAIDs lower PGE2, raising H+ secretion and lowering bicarbonate secretion and gastric blood flow; lines 1045–1046, Cushing and Curling ulcers); Robbins Ch. 17 (NSAIDs remove prostaglandin support for mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution, and prostaglandins reduce acid secretion). The ↔ cells in rows A, B, D, and E are inferred because the sources do not describe those values as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol is used for NSAID ulcer prophylaxis and is contraindicated in pregnancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: Curling ulcers follow burns and hypovolemia, and Cushing ulcers follow brain injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID associated ulcer treatment and prevention","isHighYield":true}],"8":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, loss of which neurons causes primary achalasia?","choices":{"A":"Excitatory cholinergic neurons","B":"Vagal sensory neurons","C":"Sympathetic adrenergic neurons","D":"Interstitial pacemaker cells","E":"Nitric oxide neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary achalasia results from idiopathic degeneration or autoimmune loss of nitric oxide producing inhibitory enteric neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax and the distal esophagus loses peristalsis. Loss of excitatory cholinergic neurons would weaken contraction. Vagal sensory, sympathetic, and pacemaker cells are not the lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia shows a bird beak sign on barium swallow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, achalasia). COMLEX: achalasia raises the risk of esophageal cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where does a B ring of the distal esophagus lie?","choices":{"A":"In the upper cervical esophagus","B":"At the squamocolumnar junction","C":"At the cricopharyngeal muscle","D":"In the middle thoracic esophagus","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction and have gastric cardia mucosa on their undersurface, whereas A rings lie above the gastroesophageal junction and are covered by squamous mucosa. Upper esophageal webs occur in Plummer Vinson syndrome. The cricopharyngeus, midesophagus, and antrum are not ring sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Schatzki ring).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki rings are associated with hiatal hernia and GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal ring). COMLEX: rings cause intermittent solid food dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"What dysphagia pattern does progressive esophageal stenosis typically produce?","choices":{"A":"Painful swallowing of liquids","B":"Solids and liquids from the onset","C":"Liquids worse than solids","D":"Solids first and later liquids","E":"Episodic dysphagia for meat"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stenosis narrows the lumen progressively, so mechanical dysphagia begins with solids and later involves liquids as the caliber narrows, with appetite and weight usually preserved unless the lesion is malignant. Dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset is the motor pattern of achalasia. Episodic meat impaction fits rings. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strictures follow GERD, caustic ingestion, or radiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: barium swallow shows narrowing at the stricture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what failure during severe retching causes a Mallory Weiss tear?","choices":{"A":"Pylorus fails to close","B":"Vagal tone is lost","C":"Gastric acid is excessive","D":"Diaphragm fails to contract","E":"LES fails to relax first"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mallory Weiss tears occur when reflex relaxation of the LES fails to precede antiperistaltic gastric expulsion, so the wall near the gastroesophageal junction is overstretched and lacerated. Pyloric closure, vagal tone, excess acid, and diaphragm contraction are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallory Weiss syndrome is associated with alcohol use and bulimia nervosa (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: these tears account for up to 10% of upper GI bleeding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"What does Candida esophagitis look like at endoscopy?","choices":{"A":"Punched out volcanic ulcers","B":"Stacked circular mucosal rings","C":"Salmon pink mucosal tongues","D":"Gray white pseudomembranes","E":"Dilated submucosal veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Candida forms adherent gray white pseudomembranes of matted hyphae, pseudohyphae, and inflammatory cells over eroded mucosa. Punched out ulcers, stacked rings, salmon pink tongues, and dilated veins are not the Candida appearance. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal candidiasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Candida histology shows pseudohyphae and budding yeast (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal candidiasis). COMLEX: infectious esophagitis causes prominent odynophagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the minimum eosinophil count on esophageal biopsy that supports eosinophilic esophagitis?","choices":{"A":"At least 2 per HPF","B":"At least 15 per HPF","C":"At least 5 per HPF","D":"At least 10 per HPF","E":"At least 50 per HPF"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis shows dense intraepithelial eosinophils, at least 15 per high power field, forming superficial clusters with spongiosis. Reflux esophagitis has only scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, so counts of 2, 5, or 10 per field are below the cutoff, and 50 per field exceeds the minimum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophilic esophagitis is driven by Th2 inflammation to food antigens (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: it is associated with atopic conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell on biopsy is the histologic hallmark of Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Paneth cells","E":"Keratinocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Barrett esophagus is simple columnar epithelium containing distinctive goblet cells with pale blue mucin vacuoles. Parietal and chief cells belong to gastric glands. Paneth cells are not the lecture hallmark. Keratinocytes would indicate squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus the Z line extends proximally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett mucosa may undergo malignant transformation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"What process replaces esophageal squamous epithelium in Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Squamous hyperplasia","B":"Gastric glandular atrophy","C":"Intestinal metaplasia","D":"Fibrous scarring","E":"Epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Persistent acid injury reprograms esophageal stem cells toward a columnar fate, so intestinal metaplasia replaces the squamous lining with acid resistant columnar epithelium. Hyperplasia keeps squamous epithelium. Atrophy, scarring, and apoptosis do not produce columnar epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is a complication of chronic GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: endoscopy shows salmon pink mucosa above the junction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, mutation of which gene is a key step in progression of Barrett dysplasia to adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"TP53","C":"BRCA1","D":"RB1","E":"VHL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"TP53 mutation is among the genetic alterations that drive Barrett dysplasia to invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma in the lecture. APC, BRCA1, RB1, and VHL are not listed for this progression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma; section 5, pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: GERD and obesity are adenocarcinoma risk factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do esophageal veins drain the blood shunted to them from the portal circulation?","choices":{"A":"Splenic vein","B":"Inferior mesenteric vein","C":"Hepatic veins","D":"Renal veins","E":"Azygos system"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, portal blood shunted at the junction enters esophageal veins that drain into the azygos system. The splenic and inferior mesenteric veins feed the portal system. Hepatic veins drain the liver. Renal veins drain the kidneys. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices are dilated submucosal veins at a portocaval anastomosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding causes hematemesis and shock.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what share of patients with cirrhosis develop esophageal varices?","choices":{"A":"About 5 to 10 percent","B":"About 15 to 20 percent","C":"About 40 to 60 percent","D":"About 80 to 90 percent","E":"About 1 to 2 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Varices develop in about 40% to 60% of patients with cirrhosis. The lower and higher ranges do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: cirrhosis commonly follows alcohol use, viral hepatitis, or steatohepatitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the immediate mortality of each episode of variceal bleeding?","choices":{"A":"About 15 to 30 percent","B":"About 1 to 2 percent","C":"About 3 to 5 percent","D":"About 50 to 60 percent","E":"About 80 to 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Each episode of variceal bleeding carries an immediate mortality of 15% to 30%, and survivors face an 80% two year risk of rebleeding. Ranges of 1 to 2 or 3 to 5 percent understate the risk, and 50 to 60 or 80 to 90 percent overstate it; the 80% figure is the two year rebleeding risk in survivors, not immediate mortality. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: variceal bleeding presents abruptly with hematemesis and melena (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: hemorrhagic shock may follow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the esophagus does squamous cell carcinoma most often arise?","choices":{"A":"Distal third","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Middle third","D":"Upper sphincter","E":"Abdominal segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises mainly in the middle third (about 50%) and upper third, whereas adenocarcinoma lies in the distal third and often invades the gastric cardia. The upper sphincter and abdominal segment are not the usual sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, anatomic distribution); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma: proximal two thirds).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: barium swallow may show an apple core lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Human papillomavirus","B":"Epstein Barr virus","C":"Cytomegalovirus","D":"Helicobacter pylori","E":"Herpes simplex virus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture notes that H. pylori infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma, and that alcohol is not a risk factor. Human papillomavirus is listed by Bootcamp as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor. Epstein Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus are not described as protective. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, major risk factors); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma risk factors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer in the US (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: adenocarcinoma shows a strong male predominance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic feature identifies esophageal squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Keratin pearls","B":"Goblet cell glands","C":"Signet ring cells","D":"Mucin vacuoles","E":"Cribriform glands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma shows nests of malignant squamous cells with keratin pearls and intercellular bridges. Goblet cells, signet ring cells, mucin vacuoles, and cribriform glands are glandular features rather than squamous ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma histopathology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma risk factors include smoking, alcohol, and hot beverages (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both esophageal cancers present with progressive dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman who grew up in rural Brazil has dysphagia to both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food at night, and a bird beak narrowing on barium swallow. Which agent most likely caused her disease?","choices":{"A":"Trypanosoma cruzi","B":"Candida albicans","C":"Herpes simplex virus","D":"Cytomegalovirus","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secondary achalasia arises from Chagas disease, in which Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the myenteric plexus; it can also follow diabetic neuropathy, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, or malignancy. Candida, herpes simplex virus, and cytomegalovirus cause infectious esophagitis with odynophagia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Chagas disease secondary achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: T. cruzi is shed in the feces of the reduviid bug (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Chagas disease). COMLEX: Chagas disease can cause megaesophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has had episodes over several years of a steak bolus sticking low in his chest, each resolving without trouble swallowing liquids. Which lesion best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Primary achalasia","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Scleroderma dysmotility","D":"Esophageal varices","E":"Distal esophageal ring"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rings cause episodic, nonprogressive mechanical dysphagia triggered by poorly chewed solid boluses, the steakhouse syndrome. Achalasia causes dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset. Cancer causes progressive dysphagia with weight loss. Scleroderma causes motor dysphagia with reflux. Varices bleed rather than obstruct. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: rings present with dysphagia and regurgitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, cracks at the corners of her mouth, and dysphagia high in her throat. Her blood count shows a microcytic anemia. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pharyngeal Zenker diverticulum","B":"Plummer Vinson syndrome","C":"Mallory Weiss syndrome","D":"Boerhaave syndrome","E":"Barrett esophagus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upper esophageal webs with iron deficiency anemia, glossitis, and cheilosis make up Plummer Vinson syndrome. Zenker diverticulum is a pouch with halitosis and regurgitation. Mallory Weiss and Boerhaave syndromes follow vomiting. Barrett esophagus is distal metaplasia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Plummer Vinson syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plummer Vinson syndrome raises the risk of squamous cell carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Plummer Vinson syndrome). COMLEX: webs occur most often in women over 40.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old man vomits repeatedly after a night of binge drinking and then vomits bright red blood. His vital signs are stable, and the bleeding stops on its own. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Ruptured submucosal vein","B":"Bleeding gastric ulcer","C":"Longitudinal mucosal tear","D":"Ulcerated squamous carcinoma","E":"Candida pseudomembranes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A Mallory Weiss tear is a longitudinal mucosal laceration crossing the gastroesophageal junction after severe retching, presenting as painful hematemesis that usually heals quickly. Variceal rupture needs portal hypertension. A gastric ulcer is not linked to the retching sequence. Carcinoma and candidiasis do not fit the sudden onset after vomiting. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Mallory Weiss syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy shows longitudinal tears near the gastroesophageal junction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: prolonged coughing is also an association.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric propulsion and retropulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-year-old man who is otherwise healthy has severe pain on swallowing. Endoscopy shows punched out ulcers, and biopsy of the ulcer edge shows multinucleated squamous cells with nuclear molding. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Herpes simplex virus","B":"Cytomegalovirus","C":"Candida albicans yeast","D":"Trypanosoma cruzi","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Herpes simplex virus causes punched out, volcanic ulcers with multinucleated squamous cells, Cowdry A inclusions, and nuclear molding at the ulcer edge, and it can occur in healthy hosts. Cytomegalovirus causes shallow linear ulcers. Candida forms pseudomembranes. T. cruzi causes achalasia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (herpes esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: herpes esophagitis is usually caused by HSV 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, herpes esophagitis). COMLEX: odynophagia is prominent in infectious esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy with asthma and eczema has repeated food impaction. Endoscopy shows stacked circular rings and linear furrows, and his symptoms persist after 8 weeks of a PPI. Which condition is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acid reflux esophagitis","B":"Eosinophilic esophagitis","C":"Herpes simplex esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Multiple Schatzki rings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis is a Th2 driven allergic disorder linked to atopy that causes food impaction, a ringed feline esophagus with furrows and exudates, and symptoms that are refractory to PPI therapy alone. Reflux esophagitis responds to acid suppression. Herpes and Candida cause odynophagia with ulcers or plaques. Schatzki rings are distal and nonprogressive and do not cause furrows, atopy, or PPI refractory symptoms. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy may show trachealization of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: infants present with feeding intolerance and vomiting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has heartburn that worsens when she lies down after meals. Endoscopy shows hyperemia, and biopsy of the lower esophagus shows elongated lamina propria papillae. Which other finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell metaplasia","B":"Keratin pearl formation","C":"Cowdry A inclusions","D":"Basal zone hyperplasia","E":"Fungal pseudohyphae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reflux esophagitis shows scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, basal zone hyperplasia above 20% of epithelial thickness, and papillae extending into the upper third of the epithelium. Goblet cells, keratin pearls, Cowdry A inclusions, and pseudohyphae are not features of reflux injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, reflux esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD risk factors include obesity, pregnancy, hiatal hernia, and smoking (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: GERD is treated with lifestyle change and a PPI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing Barrett esophagus has surveillance biopsies. The glands show rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of nuclear polarity, atypical mitoses, and gland within gland patterns, but the basement membrane is intact. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Low grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"High grade dysplasia","D":"Reflux esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High grade dysplasia shows marked atypia, rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of polarity, atypical mitoses, and cribriform architecture, while the basement membrane is not breached. Low grade dysplasia has elongated nuclei limited to the basal aspect of glands. Invasion of the lamina propria defines carcinoma. Reflux esophagitis is inflammatory. Squamous dysplasia arises in squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, progression to dysplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic reflux causes intestinal metaplasia with simple columnar epithelium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett esophagus needs endoscopic surveillance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with Barrett esophagus has a new nodule in the distal esophagus. Biopsy shows atypical glands that have breached the basement membrane and invaded the lamina propria and submucosa. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"High grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Leiomyoma of the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Once dysplastic Barrett cells breach the basement membrane and invade the lamina propria and submucosa, the lesion is invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma. High grade dysplasia lacks invasion. Squamous cell carcinoma forms squamous nests. Stromal tumors and leiomyomas are mesenchymal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma histology shows glandular tumor with intestinal differentiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: esophageal cancer presents with progressive dysphagia for solids then liquids and rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with alcoholic cirrhosis suddenly vomits a large amount of blood and becomes hypotensive. Which lesion is the most likely source?","choices":{"A":"Mallory Weiss mucosal tear","B":"Ulcerated Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Ruptured esophageal varices","D":"Candida pseudomembranes","E":"Eosinophilic esophagitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cirrhosis raises portal pressure and creates varices beneath thin squamous mucosa that rupture with massive, life threatening hematemesis. A Mallory Weiss tear needs prior retching and is usually self limited. Carcinoma, Candida, and eosinophilic esophagitis rarely cause sudden massive bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: variceal bleeding can cause melena or hematochezia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: survivors have a high risk of rebleeding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has cirrhosis and portal hypertension. Which vein carries portal blood into his esophageal collaterals at the esophagogastric junction?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal vein","B":"Paraumbilical vein","C":"Inferior vena cava","D":"Right renal vein","E":"Left gastric vein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, blood shunts from the left gastric (coronary) vein into the esophageal veins. The superior rectal and paraumbilical veins form other portosystemic collaterals. The inferior vena cava and renal veins are systemic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices form at portocaval anastomoses (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: varices also form in the proximal stomach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis has large varices in her distal esophagus. Which anatomic feature makes these vessels especially prone to rupture?","choices":{"A":"Thick muscular venous walls","B":"Low pressure within the veins","C":"Deep location in the adventitia","D":"Thin overlying squamous mucosa","E":"Dense surrounding fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Varices lie immediately beneath thin squamous mucosa under high hydrostatic pressure, so they rupture easily and bleed massively. Their walls are thin, pressure is high, and they lie in the subepithelial and submucosal plexuses rather than the adventitia. Fibrosis does not protect them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, development of collaterals and clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices lie in the subepithelial and submucosal venous plexuses of the distal esophagus (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: varices develop in many patients with cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman who grew up in northern Iran drinking very hot tea has progressive dysphagia and weight loss. Endoscopy shows an ulcerated esophageal mass. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","D":"Esophageal leiomyoma","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide, concentrated in belts such as Iran and central China, and linked to very hot beverages. Adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett mucosa. Stromal tumors, leiomyomas, and neuroendocrine tumors are not linked to hot tea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, epidemiology, risk factors, and anatomic distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zenker diverticulum is listed as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both cancers have poor five year survival.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with long-standing reflux and obesity has a mass in her distal esophagus that extends into the gastric cardia. Which histology is expected on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Nests with keratin pearls","B":"Spindle cells with KIT","C":"Small round blue cells","D":"Malignant mucin glands","E":"Sheets of lymphoid cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma, linked to GERD, Barrett esophagus, and obesity, arises in the distal third and shows mucin producing malignant glands or signet ring cells. Keratin pearls, spindle cells, small round cells, and lymphoid sheets are not the expected pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma risk factors include Barrett esophagus, smoking, GERD, and obesity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: endoscopy with biopsy confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has a midesophageal lesion in which atypical cells replace the full thickness of the surface lining but do not invade. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Barrett metaplasia","B":"Basal zone hyperplasia","C":"Reflux esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises from squamous dysplasia, also called carcinoma in situ, driven by cell cycle gene mutations. Barrett metaplasia is columnar. Basal zone hyperplasia and reflux esophagitis are reactive. Candida esophagitis forms pseudomembranes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, pathogenesis and precursor lesions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tylosis is a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: early lesions appear as gray white plaques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman swallowed lye as a child and now has slowly worsening dysphagia. Endoscopy shows a localized circumferential narrowing. Which change in the wall causes the narrowing?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric neuron loss","B":"Submucosal fibrosis","C":"Mucosal fold duplication","D":"Muscularis hypertrophy","E":"Venous plexus dilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Esophageal stenosis is incomplete luminal opening from fibrous thickening of the submucosa with atrophy of the muscularis propria and mucosal damage; acquired forms follow caustic ingestion, GERD, radiation, or systemic sclerosis. Myenteric neuron loss causes achalasia. Fold duplication is not a stenosis mechanism. The muscularis atrophies rather than hypertrophies. Dilated venous plexuses are varices. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: caustic ingestion is a risk factor for esophageal stricture (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: caustic strictures also raise squamous cell carcinoma risk (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has sudden severe chest pain after forceful vomiting. He is tachypneic and hypotensive, with crepitus in his neck and air around the mediastinum on CT. Which injury explains the air?","choices":{"A":"Full thickness wall tear","B":"Superficial mucosal tear","C":"Variceal wall rupture","D":"Candida mucosal erosion","E":"Eosinophilic microabscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Boerhaave syndrome is a transmural esophageal rupture from a sudden rise in intraesophageal pressure during vomiting or straining, so air escapes and causes pneumomediastinum, mediastinitis, and shock. A superficial tear is confined to the mucosa and does not perforate the esophageal wall, so air does not enter the mediastinum. Variceal rupture, Candida erosions, and eosinophilic microabscesses do not breach the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Boerhaave syndrome: transmural perforation, subcutaneous or mediastinal emphysema).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boerhaave syndrome usually involves the distal third of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome). COMLEX: barium for contrast esophagography may cause mediastinitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl receiving chemotherapy has odynophagia, and endoscopy shows shallow linear ulcers. Her physician suspects cytomegalovirus. Where should the biopsy target to find viral inclusions?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cells at the margin","B":"Superficial keratin layer","C":"Ulcer base endothelium","D":"Overlying pseudomembrane","E":"Normal mucosa nearby"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CMV inclusions, both nuclear and cytoplasmic, sit within endothelial cells and stromal fibroblasts in the ulcer base, so the base is sampled. Multinucleated squamous cells at the ulcer edge are the herpes finding. A pseudomembrane suggests Candida. The keratin layer and nearby normal mucosa do not hold CMV inclusions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMV inclusions are both intranuclear and cytoplasmic (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, CMV esophagitis). COMLEX: infectious esophagitis affects immunocompromised patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has esophageal adenocarcinoma arising in Barrett mucosa, and his tumor is tested for a targetable oncogene that is commonly amplified during Barrett progression. Which gene is it?","choices":{"A":"ERBB2","B":"MYCN","C":"ABL1","D":"PIK3CA","E":"BRAF"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Progression of Barrett dysplasia to adenocarcinoma involves tumor suppressor loss and oncogene amplification such as ERBB2 or KRAS, and ERBB2 (HER2) is a targetable receptor. MYCN, ABL1, PIK3CA, and BRAF are not the oncogenes listed for this progression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma; section 5, pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: high grade dysplasia in Barrett mucosa shows loss of nuclear polarity and atypical mitoses (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: tobacco and obesity raise adenocarcinoma risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has cirrhosis. Besides fixed fibrosis, which active change inside her liver raises resistance to portal flow?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic artery dilation","B":"Lower splanchnic inflow","C":"Reduced endothelin levels","D":"Sinusoidal constriction","E":"Portal vein thrombolysis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intrahepatic resistance rises from fixed fibrosis and from active sinusoidal vasoconstriction caused by less endothelial NO and more endothelin 1; splanchnic arterial vasodilation then raises portal inflow. Hepatic artery dilation is not the mechanism. Splanchnic inflow rises rather than falls. Endothelin rises rather than falls. Thrombolysis would open rather than block the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, pathogenesis of portal hypertension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal veins at portocaval anastomoses (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: cirrhosis commonly follows alcohol use or viral hepatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — vasoactive agents for variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man from western Kenya has drunk mursik, a fermented milk, for decades and now has squamous cell carcinoma of the esophagus. Which carcinogen in the drink is implicated?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Vinyl chloride","C":"Benzene vapor","D":"Asbestos fiber","E":"Acetaldehyde"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lecture lists mursik as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor because of its acetaldehyde content. Aflatoxin, vinyl chloride, benzene, and asbestos are linked to other cancers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, major risk factors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tylosis and caustic strictures are squamous cell carcinoma risk factors (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: squamous cell carcinoma is more common in African American men in Western nations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true}],"9":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which feature of the inflammatory infiltrate typifies acute reactive gastropathy?","choices":{"A":"Few or absent neutrophils","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Dense plasma cell band","D":"Gland centered lymphocytes","E":"Eosinophil microabscesses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reactive gastropathy from NSAIDs, alcohol, bile reflux, or ischemia shows edema, vascular congestion, erosions, and foveolar hyperplasia with a notable paucity or absence of neutrophils. Pit abscesses and a superficial plasma cell rich infiltrate are features of H. pylori gastritis. A deep gland centered lymphocytic infiltrate is autoimmune gastritis. Eosinophil microabscesses are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs, alcohol, smoking, and physiologic stress cause gastritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastritis general principles). COMLEX: gastritis is evaluated by H. pylori testing and upper endoscopy with biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where are H. pylori organisms concentrated in a gastric biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Deep oxyntic glands","B":"Submucosal lymphatics","C":"Muscularis propria","D":"Lamina propria capillaries","E":"Superficial mucus layer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spiral bacilli of H. pylori are concentrated in the superficial mucus layer and are seen on H&E or silver and immunostains. They do not live in the deep glands, submucosal lymphatics, muscularis propria, or lamina propria vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is a gram negative motile spiral bacillus that may be seen on silver stain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic H. pylori infection raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, at about what age does autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically present?","choices":{"A":"Around age 20","B":"Around age 35","C":"Around age 60","D":"Around age 45","E":"Around age 80"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis typically presents around age 60 with manifestations of vitamin B12 deficiency. The other ages do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune atrophic gastritis clinical manifestations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis can follow H. pylori or pernicious anemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic gastritis). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is associated with Hashimoto thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, and Graves disease (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which zone forms the deepest layer of a chronic peptic ulcer base?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrotic debris","B":"Neutrophilic infiltrate","C":"Granulation tissue","D":"Dense fibrous scar","E":"Foveolar hyperplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A peptic ulcer shows four zones from the surface down: fibrinoid necrotic debris, an active neutrophilic infiltrate, vascularized granulation tissue, and deep dense fibrous scar. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive gastropathy feature, not an ulcer zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric ulcers most often lie on the lesser curvature (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastric ulcers carry a higher malignancy risk than duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, when does peptic ulcer pain classically occur?","choices":{"A":"Within minutes of swallowing","B":"Mostly on waking each morning","C":"One to three hours after eating","D":"Steady through day and night","E":"Mainly during strenuous exercise"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peptic ulcer pain is epigastric burning or aching that often occurs 1 to 3 hours after meals or at night and is relieved by food or antacids. Pain within minutes of swallowing suggests an esophageal cause. Pain mainly on waking, steady day and night, or with exertion does not fit, because ulcer pain follows meals by 1 to 3 hours, is worse at night (usually 11 p.m. to 2 a.m.), and is relieved by food or alkali. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, clinical features of PUD); Robbins Ch. 17 (peptic ulcer pain timing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection and NSAIDs are the main causes of peptic ulcer disease (lecture SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric ulcers lead to weight loss and duodenal ulcers to weight gain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does antral H. pylori infection lead to duodenal ulcer disease?","choices":{"A":"Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis","B":"Autonomous tumor gastrin release","C":"Autoimmune parietal cell loss","D":"Increased duodenal bicarbonate output","E":"Reduced D cell somatostatin release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In duodenal ulcer disease, antral H. pylori inflammation inhibits D cell somatostatin secretion, removing the brake on G cells; the resulting uninhibited gastrin release causes parietal cell hyperplasia and greater acid delivery to the duodenum, while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis is the mechanism of NSAIDs, which inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow. Autonomous tumor gastrin release is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a gastrinoma. Autoimmune parietal cell loss causes atrophic gastritis with low acid rather than duodenal ulcers. Duodenal bicarbonate output falls rather than rises in H. pylori duodenal ulcer disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, roles of H. pylori and NSAIDs in PUD and ZES mechanism, lines 1714–1752); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peptic ulcer disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists H. pylori in about 90 percent of duodenal ulcers and about 70 percent of gastric ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase, lowering PGE2, bicarbonate secretion, and gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which gastric polyps account for up to 75 percent of all gastric polyps?","choices":{"A":"Fundic gland polyps","B":"Gastric adenomas","C":"Hyperplastic polyps","D":"Hamartomatous polyps","E":"Neuroendocrine polyps"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps account for up to 75% of gastric polyps and arise as reactive responses to chronic mucosal injury, most often chronic H. pylori gastritis. Fundic gland polyps are less common. Adenomas are up to 10% of gastric polyps. Hamartomatous and neuroendocrine polyps are not the majority. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic gastritis can cause gastric mucosal atrophy with loss of glands and metaplasia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps peak at ages 50 to 60 (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which histologic finding characterizes a fundic gland polyp?","choices":{"A":"Cystic oxyntic glands","B":"Corkscrew foveolar glands","C":"Dysplastic tubular glands","D":"Sheets of signet ring cells","E":"Lymphoepithelial lesions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fundic gland polyps are composed of cystically dilated glands lined by parietal, chief, and foveolar cells with minimal inflammation. Elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an inflamed lamina propria are hyperplastic polyps. Dysplastic glands define adenomas. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Lymphoepithelial lesions are MALT lymphoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric adenocarcinoma is the most common gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps lie in the body and fundus (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what risk of invasive adenocarcinoma do gastric adenomas larger than 2 cm carry?","choices":{"A":"Up to 1 percent","B":"Up to 30 percent","C":"Up to 5 percent","D":"Up to 60 percent","E":"Up to 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric adenomas carry up to a 30% risk of harboring or progressing to invasive adenocarcinoma when larger than 2 cm. Up to 1 or 5 percent understates the risk, and 60 or 90 percent overstates it for adenomas larger than 2 cm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection raises the risk of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: gastric adenomas are most common in men aged 50 to 60 (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which molecular subtype of intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma features TP53 mutations and RTK RAS amplifications?","choices":{"A":"Microsatellite instability","B":"Epstein Barr virus type","C":"Genomically stable type","D":"Hypermutated POLE type","E":"Chromosomal instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The chromosomal instability subtype is characterized by TP53 mutations, Wnt pathway mutations, SMAD4 mutations, and RTK RAS pathway amplifications such as ERBB2, KRAS, EGFR, and MET. Microsatellite instability and Epstein Barr virus association are separate subtypes. Genomically stable and POLE types are not described in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, pathogenesis and molecular alterations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EBV is a risk factor for gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intestinal type tumors form glands (tubular or papillary) (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"Spread of gastric adenocarcinoma to the left supraclavicular lymph node produces which named finding?","choices":{"A":"Krukenberg tumor","B":"Virchow node","C":"Sister Mary Joseph nodule","D":"Irish node","E":"Blumer shelf"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The left supraclavicular sentinel node is the Virchow node. A Krukenberg tumor is ovarian metastasis, a Sister Mary Joseph nodule is a periumbilical node, an Irish node is the left axillary node, and a Blumer shelf is a deposit in the pouch of Douglas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Krukenberg tumor). Irish node and Blumer shelf names are not used in the lecture; their sites are.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy may present with the Leser Trélat sign or acanthosis nigricans (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: gastric cancer is difficult to detect early (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where in the stomach do intestinal type adenocarcinomas mainly arise?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and greater curve","B":"Cardia near the junction","C":"Body along the greater curvature","D":"Antrum or lesser curvature","E":"Pylorus and duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma is located predominantly in the antrum or lesser curvature and forms bulky exophytic or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. Diffuse type tumors infiltrate the wall without a discrete mass. Robbins adds that the lesser curvature is involved more often than the greater curvature, so the fundus, greater curve, and cardia are not the main sites, and the duodenal bulb lies outside the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (intestinal type: lesser curvature); Robbins Ch. 17 (most distal tumors in the antrum, lesser curvature more than greater).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at an older age than diffuse type (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric malignancy can cause gastric outlet obstruction and upper GI bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which marker pattern fits the cells of gastric MALT lymphoma?","choices":{"A":"CD20 positive CD5 negative","B":"CD5 and CD23 positive","C":"CD10 and BCL6 positive","D":"CD3 positive CD20 negative","E":"CD117 and DOG1 positive"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MALT lymphoma cells express mature B cell markers CD19 and CD20, are negative for CD5 and CD10, are CD43 positive in about 25% of cases, and show light chain restriction. CD5 positivity and CD10 positivity are excluded. CD3 marks T cells. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MALT lymphoma occurs at an older age and follows persistent H. pylori inflammation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MALT lymphoma). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma is about 5% of gastric malignancies (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — mucosal specializations and MALT","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, gastrointestinal stromal tumors arise from which cells?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle myocytes","B":"Cajal pacemaker cells","C":"Enterochromaffin cells","D":"Peripheral Schwann cells","E":"Stromal myofibroblasts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GISTs are the most common abdominal mesenchymal tumor and arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemaker cells of the gut. Enterochromaffin type cells give rise to neuroendocrine tumors. Smooth muscle cells, Schwann cells, and myofibroblasts are not the described origin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIST is most common in the stomach but can arise elsewhere in the GI tract (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST occurs at an older age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which staining result supports the diagnosis of a gastric neuroendocrine tumor?","choices":{"A":"CD117 and DOG1 staining","B":"CD20 and CD19 staining","C":"Desmin and actin staining","D":"Chromogranin A staining","E":"S100 and SOX10 staining"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastric neuroendocrine tumors express synaptophysin and chromogranin A, and the diagnosis is made by histology with this positivity. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. CD20 and CD19 mark B cell lymphoma. Desmin, actin, S100, and SOX10 are not the described markers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome causes watery diarrhea, flushing, and bronchospasm (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: urinary 5 HIAA is used to diagnose carcinoid tumors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man had a partial gastrectomy with a gastrojejunal anastomosis decades ago and has chronic bile reflux. A gastric biopsy shows mucosal edema, vascular congestion, and corkscrew foveolar hyperplasia. Which diagnosis fits best?","choices":{"A":"Helicobacter gastritis","B":"Reactive gastropathy","C":"Autoimmune gastritis","D":"Menetrier disease","E":"MALT lymphoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile reflux injures the epithelium directly, and the reactive gastropathy pattern is edema, congestion, foveolar hyperplasia with corkscrew profiles, and few or no neutrophils. H. pylori gastritis has neutrophils and plasma cells. Autoimmune gastritis has a deep lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in the body. Ménétrier disease causes giant folds with protein loss. MALT lymphoma is a dense lymphoid infiltrate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: previous gastric surgery raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: management of gastritis includes avoiding NSAIDs, tobacco, and alcohol (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has chronic dyspepsia, and a silver stain of his antral biopsy shows curved rods. Which inflammatory finding marks active disease in this gastritis?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophilic microabscesses","B":"Noncaseating granulomas","C":"Deep lymphoid gland injury","D":"Mast cell infiltrates","E":"Intraepithelial neutrophils"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In H. pylori gastritis, intraepithelial and lamina propria neutrophils form pit abscesses, alongside a superficial infiltrate of plasma cells, lymphocytes, and macrophages. Eosinophilic microabscesses and granulomas are not the described pattern. Deep gland centered lymphocytic injury is autoimmune gastritis. Mast cell infiltrates are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is treated with triple therapy (amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI) or quadruple therapy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: H. pylori infection usually starts in the antrum and spreads to the body (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has a macrocytic anemia. Biopsy of the gastric body shows a deep gland centered infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells without prominent neutrophils, with goblet and Paneth cells in the mucosa. Which other change is expected in her oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Foveolar hyperplasia","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Spiral surface bacilli","D":"Loss of chief cells","E":"Signet ring cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis shows thinning of the body and fundic mucosa, a deep gland centered lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate, extensive loss of parietal and chief cells, intestinal metaplasia, and neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive or Ménétrier change. Pit abscesses and spiral bacilli are H. pylori gastritis. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune atrophic gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is linked to HLA predisposition (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a known posterior duodenal ulcer now has constant epigastric pain radiating to his back, and his serum lipase is raised. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Penetration into pancreas","B":"Free perforation","C":"Gastric outlet obstruction","D":"Acute ulcer hemorrhage","E":"Malignant transformation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Penetration of an ulcer into an adjacent structure such as the pancreas causes constant pain radiating to the back and can raise pancreatic enzymes. Free perforation causes peritonitis and free air. Outlet obstruction causes vomiting. Hemorrhage causes melena or hematemesis. Duodenal ulcers are usually benign. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (duodenal ulcers usually benign).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a posterior duodenal ulcer can bleed from the gastroduodenal artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, ulcer hemorrhage). COMLEX: H. pylori and gastrinoma cause duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has endoscopy for epigastric pain. A 1.5 cm round gastric lesion has clean, flat, nonelevated borders and a smooth base, and biopsy shows necrotic debris over granulation tissue and scar. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal type carcinoma","B":"Ulcerated stromal tumor","C":"Gastric MALT lymphoma","D":"Linitis plastica","E":"Benign peptic ulcer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A peptic ulcer is classically a solitary, small, round to oval punched out defect with clean nonelevated borders and a smooth flat base, with zones of necrotic debris, neutrophils, granulation tissue, and scar. Intestinal type carcinoma forms bulky or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. A stromal tumor is a submucosal mass. MALT lymphoma is a lymphoid infiltrate. Linitis plastica thickens the whole wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD; section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs and H. pylori cause gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: PUD is managed with a PPI or H2 antagonist (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with years of ulcer disease near the pylorus now vomits undigested food several hours after meals, and a succussion splash is heard. Which change most likely causes her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","B":"Vagal motor neuron loss","C":"Twisting of the stomach","D":"Pyloric channel scarring","E":"Swallowed bezoar formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peptic ulcers can cause gastric outlet obstruction from edema or fibrous scarring. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is a congenital infant disorder. Vagal motor neuron loss, gastric volvulus, and bezoars are not ulcer complications in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric outlet obstruction is a complication of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intractable pain is also a complication of PUD (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing, propulsion, and pyloric resistance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl whose father had colectomy for hundreds of colon adenomas has many small polyps in her gastric body and fundus made of cystically dilated glands. Which gene is most likely mutated in her germline?","choices":{"A":"CDH1","B":"STK11","C":"APC","D":"SMAD4","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Syndromic fundic gland polyps arise with germline APC mutations in familial adenomatous polyposis, which is dominant and fits her father; they can develop dysplasia. CDH1 causes hereditary diffuse gastric cancer. STK11 and SMAD4 cause hamartomatous polyposis syndromes. KRAS is a somatic oncogene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps). The STK11 and SMAD4 syndromes are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are the most common colonic polyps (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, colonic polyps). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps can also arise with MUTYH mutations (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has taken omeprazole for ten years. Endoscopy shows several small smooth polyps in the gastric body, and biopsy confirms the polyp type linked to his acid suppression. What is their malignant potential?","choices":{"A":"Negligible cancer risk","B":"Risk similar to adenomas","C":"High risk needing gastrectomy","D":"Risk rising after 1 cm","E":"Frequent HER2 amplification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sporadic and PPI associated fundic gland polyps carry no significant risk of malignant transformation, unlike syndromic polyps in FAP. Adenomas carry a high risk. Hyperplastic polyp risk rises in pedunculated polyps over 1 cm. HER2 amplification is a carcinoma change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps; hyperplastic polyps; gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sporadic fundic gland polyps have increased with widespread PPI use (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — drug classes for acid suppression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic atrophic gastritis. Endoscopy shows a solitary 2.5 cm velvety antral polyp, and biopsy shows enlarged elongated hyperchromatic nuclei with pseudostratification and crowded glands. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Repeat biopsy in five years","B":"Complete endoscopic resection","C":"PPI therapy without resection","D":"Reassurance and no follow up","E":"Antibiotics without resection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is a gastric adenoma, a true neoplastic polyp with epithelial dysplasia that arises on chronic gastritis with atrophy and metaplasia; its risk of harboring or progressing to invasive carcinoma requires complete endoscopic resection and surveillance. Delayed biopsy, acid suppression, reassurance, or antibiotics leave the dysplastic lesion in place. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy is diagnosed by endoscopy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: high grade dysplasia carries a substantial risk of progression to invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Barrett esophagus and dysplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has early satiety and weight loss. Imaging shows a diffusely thickened, rigid stomach wall without a discrete mass, and biopsy shows single discohesive cells infiltrating the wall. What mainly stiffens the wall?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal edema","B":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy","C":"Amyloid deposition","D":"Desmoplastic stroma","E":"Dense lymphoid infiltrate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma infiltrates as individual mucin filled cells and elicits an intense desmoplastic stromal reaction that stiffens and thickens the wall without a mass, giving the leather bottle appearance of linitis plastica. Edema, muscle hypertrophy, amyloid, and lymphoid infiltrates are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma has obscure borders and poorly cohesive cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: early satiety is especially common in diffuse type cancer (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man grew up eating salt preserved and smoked fish and has smoked for decades. He now has a bulky antral adenocarcinoma of the intestinal type. Which dietary carcinogen class is most implicated?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Acrylamide","C":"Nitrosamines","D":"Benzene","E":"Vinyl chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors include chronic H. pylori gastritis, a high salt diet, N nitroso compounds or nitrosamines, and smoking. Aflatoxin is linked to liver cancer. Acrylamide, benzene, and vinyl chloride are not the listed gastric risk factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, risk factors and precursor lesions); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (nitrosamines, smoked and salt preserved foods).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric cancer metastasizes to the periumbilical node as a Sister Mary Joseph nodule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: tobacco use raises gastric cancer risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal cancer risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has bilateral solid ovarian masses, and the ovarian tissue contains mucin filled cells with nuclei pushed to the periphery. Her stomach wall is thickened. Which term describes the ovarian lesions?","choices":{"A":"Brenner tumor","B":"Krukenberg tumor","C":"Dysgerminoma","D":"Mature teratoma","E":"Granulosa cell tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metastasis of gastric signet ring cell carcinoma to both ovaries is a Krukenberg tumor. Brenner tumors, dysgerminomas, teratomas, and granulosa cell tumors are primary ovarian tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Krukenberg tumor). The primary ovarian tumor types are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at a younger age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric cancer can spread to the pouch of Douglas (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has early stage gastric marginal zone B cell lymphoma arising in mucosa infected with H. pylori. Which first treatment can produce complete remission?","choices":{"A":"Trastuzumab therapy","B":"Total gastrectomy","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Octreotide therapy","E":"Radiation therapy first"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Early stage H. pylori dependent MALT lymphomas frequently achieve complete remission after antibiotic eradication of H. pylori, because chronic antigen stimulation drives them. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Gastrectomy is not first line. Octreotide is a somatostatin analog for neuroendocrine tumors. Radiation is reserved for resistant tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (MALT lymphoma: eradication therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: quadruple therapy for H. pylori uses metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma presents with dyspepsia, epigastric pain, or GI bleeding (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with autoimmune gastritis has several small yellow submucosal nodules in the gastric body. Biopsy shows uniform cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and staining confirms neuroendocrine origin. Which finding best predicts an indolent course?","choices":{"A":"Low Ki 67 index","B":"TP53 and RB1 loss","C":"High mitotic count","D":"Liver metastases","E":"KIT gene mutation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Well differentiated neuroendocrine tumors have a low Ki 67 proliferative index, and small gastric NETs associated with autoimmune gastritis have an indolent course managed by endoscopic resection. High Ki 67 with TP53 and RB1 mutations marks poorly differentiated carcinoma. Liver metastases signal aggressive disease and carcinoid syndrome. KIT mutations drive GIST. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about one third of carcinoid tumors metastasize, usually to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: carcinoid tumors are managed with resection or a somatostatin analog such as octreotide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has melena. CT shows a 9 cm gastric submucosal mass with liver lesions, and biopsy shows spindle cells that stain strongly for CD117 and DOG1. Which drug is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab","B":"Trastuzumab","C":"Octreotide","D":"Cisplatin","E":"Imatinib"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Unresectable or metastatic GISTs with KIT or PDGFRA mutations are treated with tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as imatinib. Rituximab targets CD20 on B cells. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Octreotide treats neuroendocrine tumors. Cisplatin is not the described therapy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: c KIT and PDGFRA mutations cause aberrant receptor tyrosine kinase activity in GIST (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST malignant risk correlates with size, mitotic count, and location (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has leg edema and a low serum albumin. Endoscopy shows giant cerebriform folds in the gastric body, and biopsy shows marked foveolar hyperplasia with atrophy of the oxyntic glands. Overactivity of which signaling pathway causes this disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin via CCK2","B":"Histamine via H2","C":"Hedgehog via Smoothened","D":"Wnt via Frizzled","E":"TGF alpha via EGFR"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ménétrier disease results from increased TGF alpha binding the EGF receptor, which drives foveolar hyperplasia and mucus overproduction with parietal and chief cell atrophy; the protein losing gastropathy causes hypoalbuminemia and edema. Gastrin and histamine stimulate acid secretion rather than foveolar hyperplasia. Hedgehog and Wnt signaling are not the described pathway. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Ménétrier disease: TGF alpha binds EGFR, protein losing gastropathy, foveolar hyperplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ménétrier disease shows thick gastric folds resembling cerebral gyri on imaging (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Ménétrier disease). COMLEX: Ménétrier disease raises the risk of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has refractory ulcers, and imaging for a suspected gastrinoma searches his pancreas. Besides the pancreas, where do these tumors commonly arise?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Gastric fundus","C":"Splenic hilum","D":"Duodenal wall","E":"Distal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrinomas arise in the non beta cell pancreas, the duodenum, or peripancreatic soft tissue, the region called the gastrinoma triangle. Antral G cells make normal gastrin but are not the described tumor site. The fundus, splenic hilum, and distal ileum are not gastrinoma sites in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture links Zollinger-Ellison syndrome to MEN1 (lecture SDL 9, neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: gastric NETs can arise in the hypergastrinemia of Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has a gastric polyp that arose on chronic H. pylori gastritis. It shows elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an edematous, inflamed lamina propria. Which feature most raises its risk of dysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Pedunculated and over 1 cm","B":"Location in the fundus","C":"Many tiny sessile polyps","D":"Patient age under thirty years","E":"Smooth surface mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps carry a dysplasia risk of 1% to 20%, which rises significantly in pedunculated polyps larger than 1 cm. Fundic location, multiple tiny polyps, young age, and a smooth surface are not the described risk features. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection raises the risk of peptic ulcer disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps are most common in the antrum (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":3,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has a sporadic diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma that has lost E-cadherin protein, but sequencing finds no mutation in the gene that encodes it. Which change most likely silenced the gene?","choices":{"A":"Gene amplification","B":"Activating point mutation","C":"Promoter hypermethylation","D":"Balanced translocation","E":"Microsatellite expansion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma is driven by loss of the E-cadherin gene in about half of sporadic cases, either by loss of function mutation or by promoter hypermethylation, an epigenetic change that silences an intact sequence. Amplification and activating mutations increase gene activity. Balanced translocations and microsatellite expansions are not the described mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, pathogenesis and molecular alterations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma shows signet ring cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: hereditary diffuse gastric cancer arises de novo without identified precursor lesions (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — molecular progression in Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 73-year-old man has gastric MALT lymphoma. Which fusion protein is produced by the most characteristic translocation of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"API2 MALT1 fusion","B":"BCR ABL1 fusion","C":"PML RARA fusion","D":"MYC IGH fusion","E":"EWSR1 FLI1 fusion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The t(11;18)(q21;q21) translocation forms the API2 MALT1 fusion protein. BCR ABL1 is chronic myeloid leukemia, PML RARA is acute promyelocytic leukemia, MYC IGH is Burkitt lymphoma, and EWSR1 FLI1 is Ewing sarcoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas). The other fusions are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MALT lymphoma forms lymphoid follicles within the gastric mucosa (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MALT lymphoma). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma is diagnosed by endoscopic biopsy with IgH clonality testing (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — mucosal lymphoid tissue","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 13-year-old girl has a paraganglioma and a gastric stromal tumor. Tumor testing finds no KIT or PDGFRA mutation. Which defect most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"CDH1 mutation","B":"SDH deficiency","C":"APC mutation","D":"Menin gene mutation","E":"RET mutation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GISTs without KIT or PDGFRA mutations can be driven by succinate dehydrogenase complex mutations, which are linked to Carney Stratakis syndrome with paraganglioma. CDH1 causes diffuse gastric cancer. APC causes FAP. MEN1 and RET cause endocrine neoplasia syndromes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors). The paraganglioma link of Carney Stratakis syndrome is not stated in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIST mutations in KIT and PDGFRA are mutually exclusive (lecture SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric GISTs are less aggressive than small intestinal GISTs (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true}],"10":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long does acid suppression from a single proton pump inhibitor dose last despite a plasma half life of about 1.5 hours?","choices":{"A":"About 90 minutes","B":"About 6 hours","C":"About 3 days","D":"About 2 weeks","E":"About 24 hours"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors have a short plasma half life of about 1.5 hours but act for about 24 hours because they bind the H+/K+ ATPase covalently and irreversibly, so acid secretion returns only as new pumps are made. The shorter and longer durations do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which antisecretory drug class requires a dose reduction in renal insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Alginate raft formers","C":"H2 receptor antagonists","D":"Calcium carbonate antacids","E":"Dopamine D2 antagonists"},"correct":"C","explanation":"H2 receptor antagonists are cleared by hepatic metabolism and renal secretion, so their dose is reduced in renal insufficiency. Proton pump inhibitors are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal adjustment. Alginates and antacids act locally, although magnesium and aluminum accumulate in renal failure. Dopamine D2 antagonists are prokinetics rather than antisecretory drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 antagonists reversibly inhibit H2 receptors on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 antagonists are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how far apart should antacid doses be spaced from tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, or iron?","choices":{"A":"Ten minutes","B":"Six hours","C":"Twelve hours","D":"Two hours","E":"One full day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antacids bind other drugs such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, and iron, so the lecture calls for a 2 hour separation. Ten minutes falls short of that separation, and six hours, twelve hours, or a full day are longer than the lecture requires. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aluminum hydroxide causes constipation and hypophosphatemia, while magnesium hydroxide causes diarrhea (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: calcium carbonate can cause milk alkali syndrome and rebound acidity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, why is bismuth quadruple therapy now recommended as first line H. pylori treatment?","choices":{"A":"Clarithromycin resistance","B":"Amoxicillin allergy rates","C":"Shorter treatment course","D":"Fewer daily pill doses","E":"Metronidazole resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bismuth quadruple therapy is first line because clarithromycin resistance is rising, and triple therapy is now limited to regions where resistance is below 15 percent. The quadruple regimen lasts 10 to 14 days with several daily doses, so it is not shorter or simpler, and it contains metronidazole. Amoxicillin allergy is not the stated reason, and the bismuth regimen (PPI, bismuth, metronidazole, tetracycline) contains no amoxicillin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, H. pylori eradication regimens).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori quadruple therapy combines metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: bismuth also treats traveler diarrhea and can darken the stool (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antidiarrheal medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — H. pylori gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how long must a proton pump inhibitor be held before a test that confirms H. pylori eradication?","choices":{"A":"Two days","B":"Six hours","C":"Six months","D":"Two weeks","E":"Twelve hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PPIs must be held for 2 weeks before a urea breath test, fecal antigen test, or biopsy to avoid false negative results, and testing is done at least 4 weeks after the antibiotics end. Holds of six hours, twelve hours, or two days are too short to avoid false negative results, and six months is far longer than the lecture's 2 weeks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and raise bicarbonate secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sucralfate requires an acidic environment to work (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what sustained intragastric pH threshold prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clot formation?","choices":{"A":"Below pH 2","B":"Above pH 6","C":"Near pH 3","D":"Near pH 4","E":"Above pH 10"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sustained elevation of intragastric pH above 6.0 prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clotting by optimizing platelet aggregation and coagulation. Lower values still allow clot breakdown, and a pH above 10 is not a lecture target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, reduction of recurrent ulcer bleeding risk).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors raise the risk of C. difficile infection and aspiration pneumonia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are used for GI bleeding and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and area postrema do aprepitant and fosaprepitant block?","choices":{"A":"Substance P receptors","B":"Dopamine D2 receptors","C":"Muscarinic M1 receptors","D":"Histamine H1 receptors","E":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aprepitant and fosaprepitant cross the blood brain barrier and block central NK1 receptors, the receptors for substance P. Dopamine D2 receptors are blocked by prochlorperazine and metoclopramide. M1 and H1 receptors are blocked by scopolamine and the antihistamines. Dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK1 antagonists such as aprepitant act centrally and treat chemotherapy induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: NK1 antagonists are combined with a 5 HT3 antagonist and dexamethasone for highly emetogenic chemotherapy (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what property makes palonosetron better than older agents for delayed chemotherapy induced emesis?","choices":{"A":"Extra NK1 blockade","B":"Central CB1 agonism","C":"Long 40 hour half life","D":"Added D2 receptor blockade","E":"Peripheral restriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Palonosetron is a second generation 5 HT3 antagonist with higher receptor affinity and a 40 hour half life, which makes it superior for delayed CINV. It does not block NK1 or D2 receptors or act at CB1 receptors, and it acts both peripherally and centrally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, serotonin 5 HT3 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5 HT3 antagonists cause QT prolongation, headache, and serotonin syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: 5 HT3 antagonists are used for chemotherapy, postoperative, and radiation induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, aprepitant inhibits CYP3A4. What change is required in the dexamethasone given with it?","choices":{"A":"Double the dose","B":"Reduce dose by half","C":"Give it intravenously","D":"Stop dexamethasone","E":"Give it at bedtime"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aprepitant and netupitant inhibit CYP3A4 and raise dexamethasone levels, so the dexamethasone dose is reduced by 50 percent. Raising the dose, changing the route or timing, and stopping the steroid are not what the lecture describes, because dexamethasone potentiates NK1 antagonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aprepitant also lowers the warfarin INR (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: dexamethasone used as an antiemetic can cause insomnia and hyperglycemia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how does octreotide reduce portal inflow in acute variceal bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inhibits glucagon release","B":"Activates V1a receptors","C":"Blocks beta 1 receptors","D":"Releases nitric oxide","E":"Directly constricts arterioles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatostatin and octreotide cause selective splanchnic vasoconstriction by inhibiting the release of glucagon and other vasodilatory peptides, which lowers portal inflow without systemic vasoconstriction. Vasopressin and terlipressin activate V1a receptors. Beta 1 blockade is how nonselective beta blockers reduce cardiac output. Nitric oxide is a splanchnic vasodilator that worsens portal hypertension. Octreotide's vasoconstriction is indirect; direct arteriolar constriction through vascular smooth muscle receptors is the vasopressin and terlipressin mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, somatostatin and octreotide mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal portocaval veins that form esophageal varices (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding begins abruptly with hematemesis or melena and can cause hemorrhagic shock (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which drug must be given with vasopressin in variceal bleeding to offset its systemic and coronary effects?","choices":{"A":"Phenylephrine","B":"Nitroglycerin","C":"Propranolol","D":"Terlipressin","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasopressin causes severe systemic and coronary vasoconstriction with hypertension and myocardial ischemia, so it requires co administration of IV nitroglycerin. Phenylephrine and norepinephrine add vasoconstriction. Propranolol is contraindicated during active bleeding. Terlipressin is a V1 agonist with greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects, not a drug that offsets vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vasopressin also causes free water retention and hyponatremia (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: terlipressin has greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects than vasopressin (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, to what resting heart rate is propranolol titrated in chronic variceal management?","choices":{"A":"40 to 45 bpm","B":"70 to 75 bpm","C":"80 to 85 bpm","D":"90 to 95 bpm","E":"55 to 60 bpm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dose is titrated to reduce the resting heart rate by 25 percent or to 55 to 60 bpm. Lower targets risk symptomatic bradycardia, and higher ones give too little portal pressure reduction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TIPS is a management option for portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: lactulose and rifaximin treat hepatic encephalopathy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has heartburn four times a week for six months. Endoscopy shows erosive esophagitis without Barrett mucosa. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Famotidine as needed","B":"Metoclopramide daily","C":"Antacids as needed","D":"Alginate after meals","E":"Daily omeprazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Frequent heartburn on 3 or more days a week and erosive esophagitis call for a proton pump inhibitor, which heals 85 to 90 percent of erosive esophagitis when taken 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast. H2 blockers heal less than half and suit mild infrequent symptoms. Metoclopramide is not recommended as monotherapy. Antacids and alginates give only short temporary relief. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI and H2 blocker indications).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors reduce absorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium and can cause acute interstitial nephritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: GERD is managed with lifestyle changes and a proton pump inhibitor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophagus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man takes an over the counter drug for heartburn. Over three months he develops tender breast enlargement, and his warfarin effect rises. Which drug is he most likely taking?","choices":{"A":"Pantoprazole","B":"Famotidine","C":"Cimetidine","D":"Sucralfate","E":"Nizatidine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cimetidine inhibits androgen binding and raises prolactin, causing gynecomastia, impotence, and galactorrhea, and it inhibits CYP1A2, 2C9, 2D6, and 3A4, which raises warfarin levels. Famotidine and nizatidine lack these effects. Pantoprazole is a PPI without antiandrogen effects. Sucralfate is a local mucosal protectant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine causes CYP450 inhibition, antiandrogenic effects, and CNS effects (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 blockers can cause confusion in elderly or ICU patients with renal or hepatic impairment (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with long standing diabetes has persistent regurgitation and delayed gastric emptying despite a proton pump inhibitor. An adjunct drug is added that blocks central and peripheral dopamine receptors. Which adverse effect most limits its long term use?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic diarrhea","B":"Tardive dyskinesia","C":"Milk alkali syndrome","D":"Interstitial nephritis","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist that crosses the blood brain barrier, and central D2 blockade causes extrapyramidal effects including dystonia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia. Osmotic diarrhea is from magnesium antacids. Milk alkali syndrome is from excess calcium. Interstitial nephritis and hypomagnesemia are long term PPI risks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, prokinetic agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide raises LES tone and gastric contractions and causes hyperprolactinemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide is contraindicated in small bowel obstruction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman who is 12 weeks pregnant has rheumatoid arthritis and a past bleeding ulcer. She must continue an NSAID and needs ulcer prophylaxis. Which drug should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Omeprazole","B":"Famotidine","C":"Pantoprazole","D":"Misoprostol","E":"Sucralfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Misoprostol is a synthetic PGE1 analog that raises mucus and bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow, but it is an abortifacient and is contraindicated in pregnancy, and it also causes dose dependent cramping and diarrhea. A once daily PPI is the alternative cotherapy for high risk NSAID users. Famotidine and sucralfate act on acid or the mucosa without abortifacient effects. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, NSAID ulcer prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol raises bicarbonate and mucus, lowers parietal cell acid secretion, and raises gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: misoprostol is used for NSAID induced PUD prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a duodenal ulcer and a positive urea breath test starts a regimen of two antimicrobials, bismuth, and a twice daily acid suppressant. How does the acid suppressant improve eradication?","choices":{"A":"Antibiotic stability","B":"Thicker protective mucus","C":"Bacterial urease blockade","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Stronger IgA secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PPIs suppress acid and raise gastric pH, which increases antibiotic stability and lowers the minimal inhibitory concentration against H. pylori. They do not thicken mucus, block urease directly, speed emptying, or raise IgA. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori triple therapy combines amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: after the antibiotics, the PPI is continued once daily for 4 to 6 weeks to heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has hematemesis. Endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer with a visible vessel that is treated endoscopically. Which drug regimen best lowers his risk of rebleeding?","choices":{"A":"Oral famotidine at bedtime","B":"IV PPI bolus and infusion","C":"IV octreotide infusion","D":"Oral sucralfate slurry","E":"IV vasopressin infusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ulcers with high risk stigmata such as active bleeding, a visible vessel, or an adherent clot are treated with an IV PPI bolus followed by a continuous infusion for 72 hours, or high dose oral PPI. Bedtime famotidine and sucralfate give too little acid suppression. Octreotide and vasopressin are vasoactive drugs for variceal bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, high dose or IV PPI therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peptic ulcer disease is a cause of upper GI bleeding along with varices and Mallory Weiss tears (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, upper GI bleeding). COMLEX: a duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who is 10 weeks pregnant has nausea and vomiting most mornings. Diet changes have not helped. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Meclizine by mouth","B":"Dronabinol by mouth","C":"Prochlorperazine suppository","D":"Aprepitant with dexamethasone","E":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine is the H1 antihistamine combination the lecture names for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy. Meclizine is for motion sickness and vertigo. Dronabinol is second line for refractory CINV. Prochlorperazine is a broad spectrum D2 antagonist with extrapyramidal effects. Aprepitant with dexamethasone is for highly emetogenic CINV. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics cause sedation, dry mouth, blurred vision, and urinary retention (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: prochlorperazine blocks D2, muscarinic, and histamine receptors and causes extrapyramidal symptoms (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is leaving on a week long sailing trip and became very nauseated on his last boat trip. Which option best prevents his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Scopolamine patch","B":"Ondansetron tablets","C":"Aprepitant capsules","D":"Dexamethasone tablets","E":"Dronabinol capsules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Motion sickness is prevented by blocking M1 and H1 receptors in the vestibular apparatus and solitary nucleus, as with a transdermal scopolamine patch, meclizine, or dimenhydrinate. 5 HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are ineffective for motion sickness. Aprepitant is for CINV, and dexamethasone potentiates other antiemetics in CINV and PONV. Dronabinol is for refractory CINV and AIDS cachexia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists that can cause euphoria, sedation, and orthostatic hypotension (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: cannabinoids also stimulate appetite in AIDS cachexia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man taking sertraline for depression receives repeated doses of ondansetron after surgery. He becomes agitated with tremor, clonus, sweating, and a temperature of 39.2 °C. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonia","B":"Serotonin syndrome","C":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","D":"Anticholinergic toxicity","E":"Thyroid storm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"5 HT3 antagonists carry a risk of serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs or SNRIs, and clonus, tremor, agitation, and fever fit it. Acute dystonia is an extrapyramidal effect of D2 blockers. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows dopamine blockade and causes rigidity rather than clonus. Anticholinergic toxicity causes dry skin rather than sweating. Thyroid storm is not linked to these drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, 5 HT3 antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide blocks D2 and 5 HT3 receptors centrally and stimulates 5 HT4 receptors peripherally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: metoclopramide can cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — GI hormones, paracrines, and neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with alcohol related cirrhosis has hematemesis, a pulse of 118/min, and a blood pressure of 88/54 mm Hg. An infusion of octreotide is started before endoscopy. Why is his usual propranolol held?","choices":{"A":"Blunts compensatory reflexes","B":"Raises portal vein inflow","C":"Worsens hepatic encephalopathy","D":"Interacts with octreotide","E":"Constricts coronary arteries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers are contraindicated in acute active variceal bleeding because they blunt the compensatory cardiovascular reflexes needed during hemorrhagic shock. They lower rather than raise portal inflow. Encephalopathy and an octreotide interaction are not the reason given. Coronary vasoconstriction is a vasopressin effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, contraindications and adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the esophageal portocaval anastomosis joins the left gastric vein to the distal esophageal veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: octreotide is given immediately for suspected variceal bleeding and continued for 3 to 5 days (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis and medium varices that have never bled starts nadolol. Besides lowering cardiac output, which effect of the drug reduces portal inflow?","choices":{"A":"Direct V1a agonism","B":"Reduced glucagon release","C":"Unopposed alpha 1 tone","D":"More nitric oxide release","E":"Stellate cell relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers lower portal pressure by beta 1 blockade, which cuts cardiac output, and by beta 2 blockade of vasodilatory receptors, which leaves alpha 1 receptors unopposed to constrict the splanchnic bed. V1a agonism is the vasopressin mechanism. Reduced glucagon release is the octreotide mechanism. Nitric oxide dilates the splanchnic bed. Stellate cell relaxation is not the described mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, nonselective beta blocker mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: beta blockers are contraindicated in severe asthma or COPD because of bronchospasm (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with cirrhosis survived a variceal bleed two months ago and takes propranolol. What should be combined with it to prevent another bleed?","choices":{"A":"Chronic octreotide infusion","B":"Daily oral metoprolol","C":"Monthly vasopressin doses","D":"Endoscopic band ligation","E":"Balloon tamponade"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary prophylaxis combines a nonselective beta blocker with endoscopic variceal band ligation. Octreotide and vasopressin are short term acute therapies. Metoprolol is cardioselective and far less effective. Balloon tamponade is a temporary rescue measure for active bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement assesses portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: primary prophylaxis is for medium or large varices that have not bled (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on hemodialysis takes an antacid several times a day for heartburn and has had loose stools since starting it. He develops weakness, hyporeflexia, and hypotension. Which ion has most likely accumulated?","choices":{"A":"Calcium","B":"Aluminum","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Phosphate","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Magnesium and aluminum from antacids accumulate in renal failure, and magnesium excess causes hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure. Calcium excess follows calcium carbonate use and can cause milk alkali syndrome. Aluminum also accumulates in renal failure but causes constipation rather than loose stools. Aluminum hydroxide lowers phosphate. Bicarbonate excess follows sodium bicarbonate use. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastric pharmacology, lines 1203–1209).","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium hydroxide can cause hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: antacids give rapid relief within 5 to 15 minutes that lasts only 1 to 2 hours (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has taken a proton pump inhibitor daily for eight years and now has a low trauma hip fracture. Which drug effect best explains her fracture risk?","choices":{"A":"Reduced vitamin D synthesis","B":"Reduced calcium absorption","C":"Increased renal calcium loss","D":"Direct osteoclast activation","E":"Low estrogen production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long term PPI use decreases absorption of calcium, non heme iron, and vitamin B12 and raises the risk of osteoporotic hip and wrist fractures. PPIs do not block vitamin D synthesis, raise renal calcium loss, activate osteoclasts directly, or lower estrogen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: long term PPI use also causes benign fundic gland polyps (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: PPIs are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal dose change (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with severe hip osteoarthritis had a bleeding gastric ulcer last year. She needs long term anti inflammatory treatment. Which regimen carries the lowest risk of another ulcer?","choices":{"A":"Celecoxib with a PPI","B":"Naproxen with famotidine","C":"Ibuprofen with sucralfate","D":"Celecoxib with antacids","E":"Aspirin with misoprostol"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Selective COX 2 inhibitors combined with a PPI carry the lowest ulcer risk in high risk patients. Famotidine and sucralfate give less protection than a PPI with nonselective NSAIDs. Antacids give only brief relief. Misoprostol protects but aspirin remains a nonselective COX inhibitor, and misoprostol causes cramping and diarrhea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for NSAID ulcers include prior ulcer or bleeding, advanced age, and anticoagulant, antiplatelet, or corticosteroid use (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: if NSAIDs are stopped, H2 blockers or PPIs for 4 to 8 weeks heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman starting cisplatin receives four drug antiemetic prophylaxis that includes an atypical antipsychotic. Which receptor types does this added drug block?","choices":{"A":"Histamine and muscarinic","B":"Cannabinoid and opioid","C":"Neurokinin and GABA","D":"Adrenergic and glutamate","E":"Dopamine and serotonin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olanzapine is part of four drug high risk CINV prophylaxis and blocks D2, 5 HT2C, and 5 HT3 receptors. Histamine and muscarinic blockade is the antihistamine and anticholinergic mechanism. Cannabinoids are CB1 agonists. NK1 antagonists block substance P but not GABA. Adrenergic and glutamate receptors are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, dopamine D2 antagonists and olanzapine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D2 antagonist antiemetics cause extrapyramidal symptoms, sedation, hyperprolactinemia, and QT prolongation (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: dexamethasone potentiates 5 HT3 and NK1 antagonists in CINV and PONV (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with cirrhosis has large varices that have never bled. He also has severe asthma with frequent attacks. Which effect makes propranolol a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Bronchospasm","B":"Hyperkalemia","C":"Hepatotoxicity","D":"Hyperglycemia","E":"Tachycardia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers block bronchial beta 2 receptors and can cause bronchospasm, so they are contraindicated in severe asthma or COPD. Hyperkalemia and hepatotoxicity are not among the lecture-listed adverse effects, they can mask hypoglycemia rather than cause hyperglycemia, and they cause bradycardia rather than tachycardia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, contraindications and adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta 1 blockade lowers cardiac output and portal inflow (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: cardioselective beta blockers such as atenolol and metoprolol are far less effective for varices (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis is given a vasopressin infusion for variceal bleeding. Two days later he is confused, and his serum sodium is 124 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Renal sodium wasting","B":"Adrenal insufficiency","C":"Osmotic diuresis","D":"Free water retention","E":"Gastrointestinal sodium loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vasopressin causes free water retention that produces hyponatremia, along with systemic and coronary vasoconstriction. Renal sodium wasting, adrenal insufficiency, osmotic diuresis, and GI sodium loss are not the described effects of vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is given as a 50 mcg bolus then 50 mcg per hour (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: long term octreotide can cause gallstones (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true}],"11":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which tissue secretes the FGF2 that starts liver bud outgrowth?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum","B":"Paraxial mesoderm","C":"Cardiac mesoderm","D":"Surface ectoderm","E":"Neural crest"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 comes from the cardiac mesoderm and nearby vascular endothelial cells at the site of liver bud outgrowth. The septum transversum secretes BMPs, not FGF2. Ectoderm is one of the repressing tissues. Paraxial mesoderm and neural crest are not FGF2 sources for liver induction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2169–2174); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 731–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver parenchyma arises from foregut endoderm, and its connective tissue comes from mesoderm of the septum transversum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: endoderm forms the primitive gut tube, the GI tract, and the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the main role of the BMPs secreted by the septum transversum during liver induction?","choices":{"A":"Raising endoderm competence","B":"Repressing hepatic genes","C":"Forming bile ducts directly","D":"Inducing pancreatic buds","E":"Blocking FGF2 signaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of prospective liver endoderm to respond to FGF2. Hepatic genes are repressed by ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and the notochord. BMPs do not form bile ducts, induce the pancreatic buds, or block FGF2. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, BMPs, lines 2175–2177); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 735–737.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ventral mesogastrium gives rise to the liver, lesser omentum, and falciform ligament (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the liver weighs about 10 percent of body weight at week 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which transcription factors control differentiation of liver bud cells into hepatocytes and biliary cells?","choices":{"A":"PAX4 and PAX6","B":"HNF3 and HNF4","C":"SOX2 and CDXC","D":"PDX1 and SHH","E":"NKX2 and GATA4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the hepatic field is specified, liver bud cells become hepatocyte and biliary lineages under control of the hepatocyte nuclear transcription factors HNF3 and HNF4. PAX4 and PAX6 specify pancreatic endocrine cells. SOX2 specifies esophagus and stomach and CDXC the intestine. PDX1 is the pancreatic factor, and SHH must be repressed for pancreas to form. NKX2 and GATA4 are not the described factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, downstream differentiation, lines 2178–2181); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 738–740.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SOX2 specifies the esophagus and stomach, PDX1 the duodenum, and CDXC the small and large intestine (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the bare area of the liver touches the central tendon of the diaphragm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of extrahepatic biliary atresia cases are the perinatal form?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 95 percent","E":"About 80 percent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The perinatal form makes up about 80 percent of cases. The tree forms normally and is then destroyed after birth by suspected viral, toxic, or autoimmune injury. The fetal form is less common and reflects abnormal development or failed recanalization. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, perinatal and fetal forms, lines 2187–2193).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia is a fibro obliterative disease of the extrahepatic biliary tree (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia shows raised AST, ALT, GGT, and ALP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, by what age does uncorrected extrahepatic biliary atresia lead to secondary biliary cirrhosis?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 weeks","B":"2 to 3 years","C":"8 to 10 years","D":"3 to 6 months","E":"12 to 18 months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncorrected obstruction leads to secondary biliary cirrhosis by 3 to 6 months of age, which is why drainage must be restored early. At 1 to 2 weeks physiologic newborn jaundice is still resolving, and 2 to 3 years or 8 to 10 years are far later than the 3 to 6 months by which cirrhosis is already present. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, morphologic features, lines 2194–2197); Robbins Ch. 18 (physiologic jaundice of the newborn resolves by 2 weeks).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ultrasound in biliary atresia shows an absent or irregular gallbladder or common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: infants present within 2 to 6 weeks of birth with neonatal cholestasis (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, extrahepatic biliary atresia is the leading indication for which procedure in children?","choices":{"A":"Small bowel transplantation","B":"Kidney transplantation","C":"Liver transplantation","D":"Pancreas transplantation","E":"Heart transplantation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Biliary atresia accounts for one third of neonatal cholestasis and 50 to 60 percent of pediatric liver transplants. The other transplants are not linked to it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical significance, lines 2201–2203).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia is the most common reason for pediatric liver transplantation (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia). COMLEX: 15 to 20 percent of infants with biliary atresia have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which part of the pancreas forms from the ventral bud?","choices":{"A":"Tail of pancreas","B":"Body of pancreas","C":"Neck of pancreas","D":"Uncinate process","E":"Accessory duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and the inferior and posterior head. The dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior and anterior head, and the accessory duct comes from the proximal dorsal duct. The neck is part of the remaining gland, which Langman derives from the dorsal bud. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion and derivatives, lines 2220–2223); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 815–817.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dorsal pancreatic bud lies in the dorsal mesentery and the ventral bud lies next to the bile duct (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: visceral mesoderm around the pancreatic buds forms the pancreatic connective tissue (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, between which gestational weeks do the parenchyma and ducts of the two pancreatic buds fuse?","choices":{"A":"Weeks 5 to 8","B":"Weeks 2 to 3","C":"Weeks 10 to 12","D":"Weeks 16 to 20","E":"Weeks 30 to 36"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ventral bud comes to lie below and behind the dorsal bud, and their parenchyma and ducts fuse between weeks 5 and 8. The other intervals do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion, lines 2217–2219).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in about 10 percent of people the pancreatic duct systems fail to fuse and the double system persists (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: pancreas divisum is usually asymptomatic but raises pancreatitis risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, the accessory pancreatic duct of Santorini derives from which duct segment?","choices":{"A":"Distal dorsal duct","B":"Proximal dorsal duct","C":"Entire ventral duct","D":"Proximal ventral duct","E":"Common hepatic duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The accessory duct of Santorini comes from the proximal dorsal duct and either obliterates or drains at the minor papilla. The main duct of Wirsung forms from the distal dorsal duct and the entire ventral duct. The common hepatic duct is biliary. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, ductal anatomy, lines 2224–2231); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 817–822.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the main pancreatic duct and bile duct enter the duodenum together at the major papilla, the ampulla of Vater (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the accessory duct, when present, drains at the minor papilla (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which structures produce the two signals, one of them FGF2, that permit pancreatic specification of the foregut endoderm?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum and heart","B":"Neural tube and somites","C":"Liver bud and yolk sac","D":"Ectoderm and allantois","E":"Notochord and dorsal aorta"},"correct":"E","explanation":"FGF2 and activin, a TGF beta family member, come from the notochord and the endothelium of the dorsal aorta. The septum transversum and cardiac mesoderm signal liver induction. The neural tube and somites, the liver bud and yolk sac, and the ectoderm and allantois are not the sources; FGF2 and activin from the notochord and dorsal aorta repress SHH in the endoderm destined to form the dorsal pancreatic bud. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of SHH, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 843–845.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic islets develop by the ninth week and scatter through the gland (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: glucagon and somatostatin cells also arise from pancreatic parenchyma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which signal must be repressed in foregut endoderm for the pancreas to be specified?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Beta catenin","C":"Sonic hedgehog","D":"Notch receptor","E":"Fibronectin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 and activin repress sonic hedgehog in the endoderm destined to form the dorsal pancreatic bud, which permits pancreatic specification. Retinoic acid, beta catenin, Notch, and fibronectin are not the described targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of SHH, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 843–845.","boardPrep":"Step 1: activin is a member of the TGF beta family (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: insulin secretion begins at about the 12th week (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":1,"stem":"According to Langman, which tissue induces the ventral pancreatic bud?","choices":{"A":"Paraxial mesoderm","B":"Visceral mesoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Cardiac mesoderm","E":"Dorsal aorta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ventral pancreatic bud is induced by visceral mesoderm, which then upregulates PDX1. Cardiac mesoderm induces the liver. Paraxial mesoderm, surface ectoderm, and the dorsal aorta are not the described inducers of the ventral bud. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 845–846; Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PDX1 upregulated under mesodermal influence, lines 2238–2239).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cells expressing both PAX4 and PAX6 become beta, delta, and gamma cells (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: cells expressing PAX6 alone become alpha cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas?","choices":{"A":"Annular pancreas","B":"Ectopic pancreas","C":"Pancreatic agenesis","D":"Pancreatic hypoplasia","E":"Pancreas divisum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly, with a 3 to 10 percent incidence. Annular pancreas and ectopic pancreas are less common, and agenesis from PDX1 loss is rare. Hypoplasia is not a listed anomaly. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2264–2266).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreas divisum is failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral bud duct networks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: in pancreas divisum the ventral and dorsal parts fail to fuse at 7 weeks (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which part of the duodenum does an annular pancreas encircle?","choices":{"A":"First part","B":"Third part","C":"Fourth part","D":"Second part","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A bifid ventral bud whose left portion migrates the wrong way forms a ring of normal pancreas around the second part of the duodenum. The first part and bulb lie proximal, and the third and fourth parts lie distal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2254–2258).","boardPrep":"Step 1: annular pancreas is associated with polyhydramnios and feeding regurgitation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: about one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where is ectopic pancreatic tissue most often found?","choices":{"A":"Stomach and duodenum","B":"Esophagus and colon","C":"Spleen and liver","D":"Ileum and appendix","E":"Gallbladder and lung"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of autopsies, most often in the submucosa or mucosa of the stomach and duodenum, then the jejunum, Meckel diverticulum, and ileum. The esophagus and colon, spleen and liver, and gallbladder and lung are not on the lecture's list of favored sites (stomach, duodenum, jejunum, Meckel diverticulum, ileum). Langman also stresses gastric mucosa and Meckel diverticulum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, ectopic pancreas, lines 2274–2278); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 873–876.","boardPrep":"Step 1: accessory pancreatic tissue can lie anywhere from the distal esophagus to the tip of the primary intestinal loop (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of autopsies (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman at 7 weeks of gestation asks how her embryo forms a liver. Ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and the notochord all release signals that reach the foregut endoderm. What is their effect on that endoderm?","choices":{"A":"Activation of liver genes","B":"Conversion to pancreas","C":"Induction of bile ducts","D":"Repression of liver genes","E":"Promotion of sinusoids"},"correct":"D","explanation":"All foregut endoderm can express liver genes, but signals from ectoderm, noncardiac mesoderm, and especially the notochord repress them. Liver forms only where FGF2 lifts this repression, so the induction works by removing a repressor. These signals do not activate liver genes, convert endoderm to pancreas, or build ducts or sinusoids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, repressor signals, lines 2163–2168); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–731 and Fig. 15.21 legend.","boardPrep":"Step 1: induction can occur by removal of a repressor signal rather than direct activation (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the liver bud appears in the middle of the third week at the distal foregut (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman at 6 weeks of gestation asks why only one region of her embryo's gut forms a liver. Her physician describes an experiment in which endoderm from the future stomach region of the gut tube is placed beside the tissue that normally induces the liver bud, and the transplanted endoderm begins to express liver-specific genes. Which property of the endoderm does this experiment show?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic potential limited to the liver bud","B":"Reprogramming by migrating mesoderm cells","C":"Fusion with septum transversum cells","D":"Hepatic potential acquired from ectoderm","E":"Hepatic potential throughout the foregut"},"correct":"E","explanation":"All foregut endoderm inherently has the potential to express liver-specific genes, so endoderm from another foregut region forms liver once it receives the inductive signal. If hepatic potential were limited to the liver bud, the transplanted stomach-region endoderm could not respond. The hepatocytes arise from the endoderm itself, not from reprogrammed mesoderm or fusion with septum transversum cells, which instead contribute BMPs, connective tissue, and Kupffer cells. Ectoderm releases repressing signals rather than conferring hepatic potential. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, foregut endoderm potential and inductive signaling, lines 2162–2177); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver bud arises as an outgrowth of endodermal epithelium at the distal foregut (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: hepatic induction depends on signals from the cardiac mesoderm, nearby endothelium, and septum transversum (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks of gestation asks how her embryo's liver began. Her physician explains that, a few weeks earlier, FGF2 released beside the ventral foregut started liver bud formation in that endoderm. By which mechanism did FGF2 start hepatic gene expression?","choices":{"A":"Inducing sonic hedgehog in endoderm","B":"Blocking repressor signals","C":"Converting nearby mesoderm into hepatocytes","D":"Stimulating PDX1 expression in endoderm","E":"Promoting apoptosis of endoderm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"FGF2 from the cardiac mesoderm and nearby endothelial cells suppresses the action of repressor signals, derepressing the hepatic field so that the foregut endoderm expresses liver-specific genes; BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of the endoderm to respond. Sonic hedgehog induction is not the hepatic mechanism, and repression of SHH is instead a step in pancreatic specification. Hepatocytes come from endoderm, not from converted mesoderm. PDX1 is the master factor for the pancreas rather than the liver. Apoptosis of endoderm creates vacuoles during duodenal recanalization and does not specify liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2169–2177; section 4, SHH repression and PDX1, lines 2235–2241); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 727–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in liver induction, BMPs from the septum transversum enhance the competence of the endoderm to respond to FGF2 (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: HNF3 and HNF4 then direct hepatocyte and biliary differentiation of liver bud cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old boy has jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and hepatomegaly. Direct bilirubin and GGT are high. Ultrasound shows no visible gallbladder. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Physiologic jaundice","B":"Breast milk jaundice","C":"Dubin Johnson syndrome","D":"Biliary atresia","E":"Crigler Najjar syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistent conjugated jaundice with acholic stools, dark urine, hepatomegaly, and an absent gallbladder 2 to 6 weeks after birth fits extrahepatic biliary atresia. Physiologic and breast milk jaundice and Crigler Najjar syndrome raise unconjugated bilirubin with normal stools. Dubin Johnson syndrome raises conjugated bilirubin but has normal stools, a visible gallbladder, and a dark liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical presentation, lines 2198–2200); First Aid 2025 GI (biliary atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia often presents after 2 weeks of life with jaundice, dark urine, and acholic stools (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia accounts for one third of neonatal cholestasis (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — conjugated versus unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-week-old girl has persistent conjugated jaundice and pale stools. An intraoperative cholangiogram shows fibrotic, obliterated extrahepatic bile ducts. Which treatment should be done now to restore bile drainage?","choices":{"A":"Ursodeoxycholic acid therapy","B":"Exchange transfusion","C":"Cholecystectomy","D":"Phototherapy sessions","E":"Kasai portoenterostomy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A Kasai portoenterostomy restores bile drainage before irreversible cirrhosis develops. Ursodiol does not open an obliterated tree. Exchange transfusion and phototherapy treat unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Cholecystectomy does not bypass the blocked ducts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, clinical significance, lines 2203–2205).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the fetal form of biliary atresia reflects failed ductal recanalization (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: intrahepatic biliary duct atresia is rare and may follow fetal infection (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old boy has rising direct bilirubin, acholic stools, and hepatomegaly, and ultrasound shows an irregular gallbladder. A liver biopsy is taken before surgery. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal ground glass cells","B":"Portal ductular reaction","C":"Centrilobular necrosis","D":"Interface plasma cells","E":"Copper laden hepatocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Biopsy in extrahepatic biliary atresia shows portal edema, ductular reactions, and neutrophilic infiltrates, with progressive fibrosis of the extrahepatic ducts. Ground glass hepatocytes suggest hepatitis B. Centrilobular necrosis suggests ischemia or toxins. Interface plasma cells suggest autoimmune hepatitis. Copper laden hepatocytes suggest Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 2, morphologic features, lines 2194–2196).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia causes jaundice and scleral icterus in the first weeks of life (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia). COMLEX: biliary atresia is associated with an absent or abnormal gallbladder on ultrasound (First Aid 2025 GI biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — bile flow through canaliculi and ducts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a pancreaticoduodenectomy, and the surgeon notes that the common bile duct runs behind the duodenum. Which developmental event explains this position?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal rotation","B":"Liver bud regression","C":"Gallbladder descent","D":"Ventral mesentery loss","E":"Splenic migration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bile duct first enters the duodenum anteriorly, but as the duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C shaped, its entrance shifts posteriorly, so the bile duct passes behind the duodenum. The ventral pancreatic bud follows the same path. The liver bud does not regress, and neither gallbladder position nor the spleen, which develops in the dorsal mesogastrium, sets where the bile duct enters the duodenum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 686–689 and 809–814; Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, rotation around the duodenum, lines 2213–2215).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas and most of the duodenum become attached to the posterior abdominal wall (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the hepatoduodenal ligament carries the portal triad (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation and the mesenteries","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has ERCP, and contrast injected at the major papilla fills the main pancreatic duct to the tail. Which embryonic ducts formed this channel?","choices":{"A":"Ventral and proximal dorsal ducts","B":"Dorsal duct and cystic duct","C":"Ventral duct and bile duct","D":"Ventral and distal dorsal ducts","E":"Proximal and distal dorsal ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The main duct of Wirsung forms from the distal part of the dorsal duct joined to the entire ventral duct, and it enters the duodenum with the bile duct at the major papilla. The proximal dorsal duct becomes the accessory duct. The cystic and bile ducts are biliary. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, main pancreatic duct, lines 2225–2228); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 817–823.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ventral bud forms the uncinate process and inferior head of the pancreas (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior head of the pancreas (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — exocrine pancreas and its ducts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has a first trimester ultrasound, and her physician explains how the fetal pancreas forms. After the ventral bud moves around the duodenum, where does it come to lie relative to the dorsal bud?","choices":{"A":"Above and in front","B":"Directly anterior to it","C":"To the left of it","D":"Behind the stomach","E":"Below and behind it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As the duodenum rotates to the right, the ventral bud moves dorsally and comes to lie immediately below and behind the dorsal bud before the two fuse. Above, in front, anterior, or left positions and a site behind the stomach do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, bud fusion, lines 2217–2218); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 812–815.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas forms from two endodermal buds of the duodenal lining (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: the tail of the pancreas lies in the splenorenal ligament with the splenic vessels (First Aid 2025 GI ligaments).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-month-old boy has a loss of function variant in a transcription factor gene, leaving only one major islet lineage able to form. Which islet hormone can still be produced normally?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon release","B":"Insulin release","C":"Somatostatin release","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide release","E":"Amylin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cells expressing both PAX4 and PAX6 become beta, delta, and gamma cells, while cells expressing PAX6 alone become alpha cells. Without PAX4, alpha cells that make glucagon can still form. Insulin and amylin both come from beta cells, somatostatin from delta cells, and pancreatic polypeptide from gamma or PP cells, and all three lineages need PAX4. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PAX4 and PAX6, lines 2242–2250); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 846–850.","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make insulin, delta cells somatostatin, and gamma cells pancreatic polypeptide (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: alpha cells make glucagon (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn girl of consanguineous parents has neonatal diabetes and fatty stools, and imaging shows no pancreatic tissue. Both alleles of one homeobox gene are inactivated. Which gene is it?","choices":{"A":"PAX6","B":"SOX2","C":"HNF4","D":"CDX2","E":"PDX1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"PDX1 is the master homeobox factor for pancreatic outgrowth and differentiation, and homozygous loss of function causes pancreatic agenesis. PAX6 specifies endocrine cells. SOX2 specifies esophagus and stomach. HNF4 controls hepatocyte and biliary differentiation. CDX2 is an intestinal factor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, PDX1, lines 2238–2241); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreatic agenesis, lines 2281–2284).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDX1 is upregulated in pancreatic endoderm under mesodermal influence (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: pancreatic agenesis is a rare anomaly with complete failure of pancreatic development (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes is 6 weeks pregnant and asks how her embryo's pancreas gets started. Her physician explains that two secreted signals from nearby tissues act on the dorsal foregut endoderm and permit it to become pancreas. One of them is FGF2. Which signal is the other?","choices":{"A":"Noggin","B":"Chordin","C":"Activin","D":"Follistatin","E":"Sonic hedgehog"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 and activin, a TGF beta family member, from the notochord and dorsal aorta endothelium repress sonic hedgehog in the foregut endoderm and so permit pancreatic specification. Noggin and chordin are BMP antagonists and are not the signals the lecture gives for pancreatic specification. Follistatin binds and neutralizes activin rather than acting with FGF2. Sonic hedgehog is the signal that must be repressed for the pancreas to form, so it is the target rather than the partner signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, repression of sonic hedgehog by FGF2 and activin, lines 2235–2237); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 826–850.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PDX1 is the master pancreatic homeobox factor, and homozygous loss causes pancreatic agenesis (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: PAX6 without PAX4 yields alpha cells, the glucagon-producing islet cells (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has recurrent pancreatitis without alcohol use or gallstones. MRCP shows most of the gland draining through a narrow minor papilla, and a small duct drains only part of the head. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Abnormal ventral bud rotation","B":"Failed ductal fusion","C":"Ectopic pancreatic rests","D":"Absent ventral bud","E":"Duodenal recanalization failure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreas divisum results from failure of the dorsal and ventral duct systems to fuse, so the dorsal bulk drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini, and about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis. Abnormal ventral bud rotation causes annular pancreas. Ectopic rests are misplaced tissue. An absent ventral bud and failed duodenal recanalization are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2264–2272).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreas divisum raises the risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: most cases of pancreas divisum are asymptomatic (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old girl vomits with every feed. Imaging shows a double bubble, and surgery finds a ring of normal pancreatic tissue around the duodenum. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Failed ductal fusion","B":"Failed duodenal recanalization","C":"Split ventral bud rotation","D":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","E":"Incomplete midgut rotation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Annular pancreas forms when a bifid ventral bud splits and its left portion migrates opposite to the right, encircling the duodenum. Failed ductal fusion is pancreas divisum. Failed recanalization causes duodenal atresia without a pancreatic ring. Pyloric hypertrophy and midgut malrotation are not ring lesions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2254–2262); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 856–862.","boardPrep":"Step 1: annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: annular pancreas can cause duodenal stenosis and complete obstruction (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a small submucosal nodule in the gastric antrum with central umbilication. Biopsy shows normal pancreatic acini, ducts, and islets. Which complication can this lesion cause?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal bleeding","B":"Duodenal atresia","C":"Biliary cirrhosis","D":"Neonatal diabetes","E":"Hepatic abscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas is usually incidental but can cause local pain, inflammation, or mucosal bleeding. Duodenal atresia is a recanalization defect. Biliary cirrhosis follows biliary obstruction. Neonatal diabetes fits pancreatic agenesis. Hepatic abscess is not a described complication. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, ectopic pancreas, lines 2276–2280).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ectopic pancreatic nests may show all the histologic features of the pancreas (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas can cause localized abdominal pain and inflammation (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 5 — normal gastric histology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand how liver development is triggered by signals from nearby tissues.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is 6 weeks pregnant by her last menstrual period, so her embryo is about 4 weeks old. In her embryo, which cells beside the gut tube at the liver bud site secrete FGF2?","choices":{"A":"Notochord sheath cells","B":"Surface ectoderm cells","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Neural crest cells","E":"Somitic mesoderm cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FGF2 that lifts repression of hepatic genes comes from cardiac mesoderm and from blood vessel forming endothelial cells next to the gut tube at the liver bud site. The notochord and ectoderm release repressing signals. Neural crest and somitic mesoderm are not described FGF2 sources. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 1, inductive signaling by FGF2 and BMPs, lines 2170–2174); Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 731–735.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac mesoderm and nearby endothelium instruct gut endoderm to express liver genes by inhibiting an inhibitor (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: liver cords intermingle with the vitelline and umbilical veins, which form the hepatic sinusoids (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the cause and clinical presentation of extrahepatic biliary atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-week-old girl has conjugated jaundice and acholic stools, and surgery finds fibrotic, obliterated extrahepatic ducts. Among infants with this defect, about what share have patent proximal ducts and a correctable lesion?","choices":{"A":"15 to 20 percent","B":"1 to 2 percent","C":"40 to 50 percent","D":"70 to 80 percent","E":"95 to 99 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Langman states that 15 to 20 percent of infants with extrahepatic biliary atresia have patent proximal ducts and a correctable defect, while the rest usually need a liver transplant. The other shares do not match. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Digestive System), SDL 1, 11 Embryo text, lines 782–785.","boardPrep":"Step 1: extrahepatic biliary atresia occurs in about 1 in 15,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: biliary atresia shows jaundice, dark urine, acholic stools, and hepatomegaly (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a mass in the tail of the pancreas on CT. Which embryonic structure formed this part of the gland?","choices":{"A":"Ventral pancreatic bud","B":"Hepatic diverticulum","C":"Cystic duct outgrowth","D":"Septum transversum","E":"Dorsal pancreatic bud"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dorsal bud forms the body, tail, and superior and anterior head of the pancreas. The ventral bud forms the uncinate process and inferior head. The hepatic diverticulum forms the liver, the cystic outgrowth forms the gallbladder, and the septum transversum gives mesoderm for the liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 3, dorsal bud derivatives, lines 2222–2223).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tail of the pancreas comes to lie against the posterior body wall in the dorsal mesogastrium (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the ventral bud lies close to the bile duct outgrowth (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify transcription factors involved in the formation of endocrine pancreatic cells.","batch":3,"stem":"Which two transcription factor genes must be expressed in pancreatic parenchymal precursors to specify the islet endocrine lineage?","choices":{"A":"HNF3 and HNF4","B":"PAX4 and PAX6","C":"SOX2 and CDXC","D":"PDX1 and SHH","E":"NKX2 and GATA4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Expression of the paired box genes PAX4 and PAX6 specifies the endocrine lineage. HNF3 and HNF4 control hepatocyte and biliary lineages. SOX2 and CDXC pattern the gut tube. PDX1 drives overall pancreatic outgrowth, and SHH must be repressed for pancreas to form. NKX2 and GATA4 are not the described factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 4, endocrine lineage specification, lines 2242–2245).","boardPrep":"Step 1: homozygous PDX1 mutations cause pancreatic agenesis (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: islet cells scatter throughout the pancreas after forming from parenchymal tissue (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman learns that MRCP shows pancreas divisum. She asks how often people with this anomaly develop chronic pancreatitis. About what share do?","choices":{"A":"About 5 percent","B":"About 25 percent","C":"About 50 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most cases of pancreas divisum are asymptomatic, but about 5 percent develop chronic pancreatitis from inadequate drainage through the minor papilla. The higher shares do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, pancreas divisum, lines 2270–2272).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in pancreas divisum the duct of Wirsung drains only a small portion of the head (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: pancreas divisum has a 3 to 10 percent incidence (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a CT for kidney stones that incidentally shows a ring of pancreatic tissue around the duodenum. About what share of people with this anomaly have no symptoms?","choices":{"A":"About one tenth","B":"About one half","C":"About two thirds","D":"About one third","E":"About nine tenths"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About one third of annular pancreas cases are asymptomatic, while others develop duodenal stenosis or complete obstruction with vomiting in neonates or adults. The other shares do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 11, section 5, annular pancreas, lines 2259–2262).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an annular pancreas forms a ring of normal pancreatic tissue around the duodenum (lecture SDL 11). COMLEX: annular pancreas can present with vomiting in neonates or adults (lecture SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true}],"12":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of total hepatic blood flow does the portal vein supply?","choices":{"A":"About 70 to 75 percent","B":"About 25 to 30 percent","C":"About 10 to 15 percent","D":"About 45 to 50 percent","E":"About 90 to 95 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The portal vein supplies about 70 to 75 percent of hepatic blood flow as deoxygenated, nutrient rich blood from the GI tract and spleen, and the hepatic artery supplies the other 25 to 30 percent as oxygen rich blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow pathway, lines 2299–2302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver has a dual blood supply from the portal vein and the proper hepatic artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy). COMLEX: the hepatoduodenal ligament carries the hepatic portal vein, proper hepatic artery, and common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, liver anatomy).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how thick are normal adult hepatocyte plates?","choices":{"A":"Two cells thick","B":"Three cells thick","C":"Five cells thick","D":"Ten cells thick","E":"One cell thick"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Adult hepatocytes form branching, anastomosing plates that are normally one cell thick and radiate from the central vein, with sinusoids between neighboring plates. Thicker plates are not the adult pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatocyte plates and sinusoids, lines 2294–2297).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes have a central round nucleus with prominent nucleoli and stain PAS positive from glycogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: hepatocytes carry out metabolism, detoxification, and protein synthesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, into which vessels does the central vein drain first?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic veins","B":"Portal venules","C":"Interlobular veins","D":"Sublobular veins","E":"Splenic veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The central vein empties into sublobular veins, which join the hepatic veins and then the inferior vena cava. Portal and interlobular veins bring blood into the lobule rather than draining it, and the splenic vein feeds the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, centripetal flow and venous drainage, lines 2303–2308).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal vein blood passes through interlobular veins and sinusoids to the central vein and hepatic vein, a summary that skips the sublobular veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: Budd Chiari syndrome causes ischemic zone 3 injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what forms the short axis of the liver acinus?","choices":{"A":"Line joining two central veins","B":"Sublobular vein branches","C":"Interlobular bile ducts","D":"Bile canaliculi network","E":"Distributing portal vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The short axis of the acinus is formed by the penetrating distributing vessels from portal triads between two classic lobules, and the long axis runs between the two nearest central veins. Sublobular veins drain the lobule, and bile ducts and canaliculi carry bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, lines 2312–2316).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 1 is the first zone to meet incoming blood and has the highest oxygen and metabolic activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is affected first by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins such as cocaine (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what gross appearance results from chronic passive congestion of the liver?","choices":{"A":"Hobnail liver","B":"Nutmeg liver","C":"Bronze liver","D":"Green liver","E":"Zahn infarct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic or congestive injury causes centrilobular necrosis and a mottled gross appearance termed nutmeg liver. A hobnail surface fits cirrhosis, bronze color fits iron overload, green color fits cholestasis, and a Zahn infarct is not the described pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2333).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is affected first by ischemia because it is least oxygenated, as in congestive hepatopathy (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and chronic alcohol use as zone 3 injuries (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what overall shape does the liver acinus have?","choices":{"A":"Diamond shaped","B":"Hexagonal prism","C":"Spherical cluster","D":"Cylindrical tube","E":"Rectangular block"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The liver acinus is a diamond or lozenge shaped functional unit defined along microvascular lines. The hexagonal prism is the classic lobule. Spherical, cylindrical, and rectangular shapes are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, line 2312).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 2 has intermediate oxygen tension and is the zone affected by yellow fever (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: the classic lobule is a hexagon with portal tracts at its six angles (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, up to what diameter are the fenestrae of hepatic sinusoidal endothelium?","choices":{"A":"About 10 nm","B":"About 10 µm","C":"About 0.1 nm","D":"About 1 µm","E":"About 100 µm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells have large fenestrae up to about 1 µm without diaphragms, wide gaps between cells, and no continuous basal lamina. The other sizes do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium, lines 2345–2349).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sinusoids are lined by a single permeable, poorly selective layer of endothelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: the apical surface of hepatocytes faces bile canaliculi and the basolateral surface faces sinusoids (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, hepatocyte microvilli in the space of Disse raise the surface area for exchange by up to how much?","choices":{"A":"Twofold","B":"Tenfold","C":"Sixfold","D":"Fiftyfold","E":"Hundredfold"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Numerous hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse and increase the surface area for metabolic exchange up to sixfold. Twofold understates the gain, and tenfold, fiftyfold, or hundredfold overstate it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, space of Disse, lines 2350–2354).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic stellate cells lie in the perisinusoidal space of Disse (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: the space of Disse lies between the sinusoidal endothelium and the hepatocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into which space to form hepatic lymph?","choices":{"A":"Space of Mall","B":"Space of Retzius","C":"Bile canaliculi","D":"Central vein lumen","E":"Canals of Hering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into the periportal spaces of Mall and forms hepatic lymph. The space of Retzius is retropubic. Bile canaliculi and the canals of Hering carry bile. The central vein carries blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2362–2363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocytes take up glucose, amino acids, lipids, and toxins from plasma in the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of total liver cells are Kupffer cells?","choices":{"A":"About 2 percent","B":"About 15 percent","C":"About 40 percent","D":"About 60 percent","E":"About 85 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kupffer cells make up about 15 percent of liver cells and 80 to 90 percent of the tissue macrophages in the body. The other shares do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, Kupffer cell location and nature, lines 2366–2370).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells are hepatic sinusoidal macrophages that carry out phagocytosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: Kupffer cells clear bacteria and damaged or senescent red cells (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, in what chemical form is dietary vitamin A stored in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Free retinol","C":"Beta carotene","D":"Retinyl esters","E":"Retinal aldehyde"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Quiescent hepatic stellate cells take up and esterify dietary vitamin A and store it as retinyl esters in cytoplasmic lipid droplets. Retinol is released bound to retinol binding protein when blood levels fall. Retinal acts in the retina, where 11-cis-retinal binds opsin, and neither it nor beta carotene is the esterified form held in stellate cell lipid droplets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, vitamin A storage, lines 2384–2388); Pawlina Ch. 18 (hepatic stellate cells: retinyl esters; 11-cis-retinal binds opsin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells store vitamin A when quiescent and produce extracellular matrix when activated (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: activated stellate cells are responsible for hepatic fibrosis (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which two cytokines drive fibrogenic activation in chronic liver injury?","choices":{"A":"IL 2 and IL 4","B":"IFN gamma and IL 12","C":"TGF beta and PDGF","D":"IL 5 and IL 13","E":"GM CSF and IL 3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"TGF beta and PDGF released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes turn stellate cells into proliferative, fibrogenic myofibroblasts. The other pairs are lymphocyte or hematopoietic cytokines not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2393).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells release IL 1, IL 6, TNF alpha, and IL 8 in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: zone 3 is usually affected first in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what diameter are bile canaliculi?","choices":{"A":"About 5 to 10 µm","B":"About 0.5 to 1 µm","C":"About 50 to 100 µm","D":"About 1 to 2 mm","E":"About 10 to 20 nm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are tiny intercellular channels about 0.5 to 1 µm across, formed by apposed grooves between hepatocytes. The other sizes do not match the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile flows from canaliculi to bile ductules, the hepatic duct, and the common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: hepatocytes secrete bile across their apical membranes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which short channels at the lobule margin are lined by both hepatocytes and cuboidal cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi","B":"Interlobular ducts","C":"Canals of Hering","D":"Spaces of Mall","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Canaliculi empty into canals of Hering at the lobule margin, which are uniquely lined by both hepatocytes and cholangiocytes and have contractile activity. Canaliculi are lined only by hepatocyte membranes. Interlobular ducts are fully cholangiocyte lined. The spaces of Mall hold lymph, and sublobular veins carry blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, intrahepatic biliary pathway, lines 2409–2412).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile ductules collect bile from the canals of Hering and are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: ductules pass into the periportal space before joining interlobular ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where do the right and left hepatic ducts unite to form the common hepatic duct?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Gallbladder neck","C":"Minor papilla","D":"Caudate lobe","E":"Porta hepatis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The right and left hepatic ducts unite at the porta hepatis to form the common hepatic duct. The ampulla of Vater and minor papilla open into the duodenum, the gallbladder neck leads to the cystic duct, and the caudate lobe is a liver lobe. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, major hepatic ducts, lines 2417–2420).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the portal triad of the lobule holds a hepatic arteriole, an interlobular vein, and a bile ductule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: tight junctions on hepatocytes prevent mixing of bile with sinusoidal blood (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a liver biopsy. At one angle of a hexagonal lobule, the pathologist identifies branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery next to an interlobular bile duct. Which other structures normally run with these?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoids and Kupffer cells","B":"Lymphatics and nerves","C":"Lymph nodes and ganglia","D":"Hepatic venules","E":"Smooth muscle bundles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A portal triad holds terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery and an interlobular bile duct, along with lymphatic vessels and nerve fibers. Sinusoids and Kupffer cells lie between the plates. Lymph nodes and ganglia are not in portal tracts. Hepatic venules lie at the lobule center. Smooth muscle bundles are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, portal triad components, lines 2291–2293).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the portal vein, hepatic artery, bile ducts, and lymphatics in the portal triad (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: the classic lobule is about 1 to 2 mm across (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has a liver transplant, and her hepatic artery thromboses while portal flow continues. Which feature of hepatic inflow has she mainly lost?","choices":{"A":"Nutrient rich blood","B":"Gut derived toxins","C":"Splenic venous return","D":"Oxygen rich blood","E":"Most of total flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hepatic artery brings oxygen rich blood and 25 to 30 percent of flow, while the portal vein brings most of the flow as deoxygenated, nutrient rich blood from the GI tract and spleen. Losing the artery removes mainly the oxygen rich supply. Gut derived blood and splenic venous return both travel in the portal vein, which still flows. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow pathway, lines 2299–2303).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic artery blood passes through hepatic arterioles and sinusoids to the central vein (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: blood from both inflow vessels opens into sinusoids at the lobule periphery (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has contrast injected into a small portal vein branch during angiography. Where does the contrast first enter the hepatic sinusoids?","choices":{"A":"Lobule center","B":"Space of Disse","C":"Lobule periphery","D":"Bile canaliculi","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery open into the sinusoids at the periphery of the lobule, and blood then flows centripetally toward the central vein. The center is where blood leaves. The space of Disse holds plasma outside the sinusoid, canaliculi carry bile, and sublobular veins drain the lobule. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow and centripetal flow, lines 2302–2306).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed blood flows centripetally from the portal triads toward the central vein (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: the hepatic veins drain into the inferior vena cava (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has cardiogenic shock for six hours. Two days later his aminotransferases are markedly raised. A liver biopsy is taken. Which pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal necrosis","B":"Midzonal necrosis","C":"Portal tract fibrosis","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Pericentral necrosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Zone 3 hepatocytes receive blood already depleted of oxygen, so shock and hypotension cause centrilobular or pericentral necrosis. Periportal injury fits direct toxins or viral hepatitis. Midzonal necrosis fits yellow fever. Fibrosis and interface hepatitis are chronic patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ischemic injury such as Budd Chiari syndrome among zone 3 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: ischemic hepatitis follows hypotension, shock, or heart failure (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow and ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has acute viral hepatitis, and a liver biopsy shows the most intense inflammation in one part of the acinus. Which part is it most likely?","choices":{"A":"Subcapsular region","B":"Midzonal zone 2","C":"Central vein wall","D":"Periportal zone 1","E":"Sublobular septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Direct acting toxins and viral hepatitis often injure the periportal zone 1, which meets incoming blood first. Zone 2 is the yellow fever pattern. The subcapsular region, the central vein wall, and sublobular septa are not described sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2338–2340).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists autoimmune hepatitis and alpha 1 antitrypsin deficiency among zone 1 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is best oxygenated and most resistant to circulatory compromise (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is exposed to carbon tetrachloride at work, and a liver biopsy shows necrosis of the hepatocytes around the central veins. Which property of these hepatocytes best explains their injury?","choices":{"A":"High P450 enzyme content","B":"Highest oxygen tension","C":"Absent Kupffer cells","D":"Thin sinusoidal flow","E":"Densest bile canaliculi network"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zone 3 has high concentrations of cytochrome P450 enzymes, so toxins that need bioactivation, such as carbon tetrachloride and acetaminophen, form reactive metabolites there and cause centrilobular necrosis. Zone 3 has the lowest, not highest, oxygen. Kupffer cells, sinusoidal flow, and canaliculi do not explain the zone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2334–2338).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is the main site of drug detoxification by CYP450 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists halothane, CCl4, and rifampin as zone 3 toxins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has normal liver function, and his hepatocytes make albumin continuously. By which route does newly made albumin reach the blood?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi to ducts","B":"Lymph to thoracic duct","C":"Canals of Hering to hepatic veins","D":"Space of Disse to sinusoid","E":"Central vein directly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse, and these enter sinusoidal blood through the fenestrated, gapped endothelium. Canaliculi and canals of Hering carry bile, lymph carries only excess Disse fluid, and hepatocytes do not secrete straight into the central vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2362).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the basolateral surface of hepatocytes faces the sinusoids (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: large macromolecules and plasma proteins pass freely from the sinusoid into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has a research liver study showing that large lipoproteins pass freely between sinusoidal plasma and hepatocytes. Besides large fenestrae and wide cell gaps, which feature allows this?","choices":{"A":"Tight endothelial junctions","B":"Thick glycocalyx layer","C":"Diaphragm covered pores","D":"Pericyte wrapped capillaries","E":"No continuous basal lamina"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The sinusoidal endothelium has large fenestrae without diaphragms, wide intercellular gaps, and no continuous basal lamina, so even large macromolecules pass freely into the space of Disse. Tight junctions, a thick glycocalyx, diaphragms, and pericyte coverage would restrict exchange. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous endothelium and exchange, lines 2345–2358).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a biopsy showing dense collagen in the perisinusoidal space and sinusoids that have lost their fenestrae. Which hepatocyte function is most directly impaired by this change?","choices":{"A":"Bile acid conjugation","B":"Plasma exchange","C":"Glycogen synthesis","D":"Drug glucuronidation","E":"Ammonia to urea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collagen in the space of Disse with loss of fenestrae, called sinusoidal capillarization, blocks the free passage of plasma between sinusoids and hepatocytes. Conjugation, glycogen synthesis, glucuronidation, and the urea cycle are intracellular functions, not the exchange step this structure provides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2358); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, sinusoidal capillarization, lines 2394–2396).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cirrhosis features bridging fibrosis from activated stellate cells and regenerative nodules (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis). COMLEX: activated stellate cells contract sinusoids and contribute to portal hypertension (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — fibrosis in chronic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a gut barrier defect that lets bacteria and endotoxin into his portal blood. Which liver cells are the first line of defense against them?","choices":{"A":"Stellate cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Pit cells","E":"Kupffer cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kupffer cells are resident sinusoidal macrophages that act as the first line of defense against gut bacteria, endotoxin, and particles in portal blood, recognizing PAMPs with pattern recognition receptors. Stellate cells store vitamin A and make collagen. Cholangiocytes line ducts. Endothelial cells line sinusoids. Pit cells are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, phagocytosis and innate defense, lines 2371–2375).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells make up 80 to 90 percent of the tissue macrophages in the body (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: Kupffer cells recognize endotoxin with pattern recognition receptors (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic hepatitis has a biopsy in which perisinusoidal cells have lost their lipid droplets, express smooth muscle actin, and lay down type I and type III collagen. Which cell type is this?","choices":{"A":"Kupffer cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Stellate cells","D":"Endothelial cells","E":"Pit cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Activated hepatic stellate cells lose their vitamin A lipid droplets, become myofibroblasts with alpha smooth muscle actin, and deposit type I and type III collagen in the space of Disse, causing fibrosis. Kupffer cells release the activating cytokines but do not make this matrix. Cholangiocytes, endothelial cells, and pit cells are not the fibrogenic cell. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells sit in the space of Disse between hepatocytes and endothelial cells (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: chronic liver injury, reactive oxygen species, and cytokines activate stellate cells (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has chronic hemolysis, and her Kupffer cells take up many senescent red cells. After hemoglobin breakdown, how is the released iron sent back to the blood?","choices":{"A":"Through ferroportin","B":"Through DMT1 channels","C":"Through hepcidin pores","D":"Through hemopexin","E":"Through haptoglobin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kupffer cells break down hemoglobin and store iron in ferritin or export it through ferroportin to bind transferrin for erythropoiesis. DMT1 is an apical iron importer, hepcidin regulates ferroportin rather than forming pores, and hemopexin and haptoglobin bind heme and hemoglobin in plasma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, iron recycling, lines 2376–2380).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells store iron safely in ferritin (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: exported iron binds transferrin for systemic erythropoiesis (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — hemochromatosis and iron overload","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has a liver biopsy, and the histology instructor traces bile flow within a classic lobule. In which direction does bile move?","choices":{"A":"Toward central veins","B":"Into sinusoids","C":"Toward portal triads","D":"Into space of Disse","E":"Into sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile flows centrifugally, starting near the central vein and moving outward to the portal triads, opposite to the centripetal flow of blood toward the central vein. Bile does not enter sinusoids, the space of Disse, or sublobular veins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, directional contrast, lines 2421–2425).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile and blood flow in opposite directions in the lobule (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: interlobular ducts converge into larger intrahepatic ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman has an autoimmune disease that destroys the small interlobular bile ducts. Where are these ducts located in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Around central veins","B":"Within portal triads","C":"In space of Disse","D":"Between hepatocyte plates","E":"In sublobular septa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile ductules join interlobular bile ducts within the portal triads of the portal canals. The central veins carry blood, the space of Disse holds plasma, canaliculi rather than ducts run between hepatocytes, and sublobular septa are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, interlobular bile ducts, lines 2415–2416).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interlobular bile ducts run in the portal canals with the portal vein and hepatic artery branches (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: intrahepatic bile ductules are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary cholangitis and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has a cholestatic drug injury in which bile leaks from canaliculi into the blood. Which structures normally seal bile canaliculi from the vascular space?","choices":{"A":"Tight junctions","B":"Desmosomes","C":"Gap junctions","D":"Hemidesmosomes","E":"Adherens junctions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between hepatocytes and are sealed from the vascular space by tight junctions. Desmosomes and adherens junctions anchor cells, gap junctions allow small molecule passage, and hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: canals of Hering have contractile activity that propels bile (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between adjacent hepatocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman has hepatic venous outflow obstruction. Normally, into which vessel do the hepatic veins empty?","choices":{"A":"Inferior vena cava","B":"Superior vena cava","C":"Hemiazygos vein","D":"Left renal vein","E":"Superior mesenteric vein"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blood drains from the sinusoids into the central vein, then sublobular veins, then hepatic veins, and finally the inferior vena cava. The superior vena cava, hemiazygos vein, and left renal vein do not receive hepatic venous blood, and the superior mesenteric vein feeds the portal vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, centripetal flow and venous drainage, lines 2306–2308).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal vein blood passes through sinusoids to the central vein and on to the hepatic vein (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: hepatic venous outflow obstruction injures zone 3 first (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man returns from South America with fever, jaundice, and bleeding, and is diagnosed with yellow fever. Which acinar zone is classically injured?","choices":{"A":"Periportal zone 1","B":"Pericentral zone 3","C":"Panlobular injury","D":"Midzonal zone 2","E":"Zone 1 and 3 together"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Yellow fever classically causes midzonal, zone 2 injury. Zone 1 is injured by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins such as cocaine. Zone 3 is injured by ischemia and bioactivated toxins. Yellow fever injury is midzonal rather than panlobular or split between zones 1 and 3. Sources: Bootcamp Gastroenterology (hepatic zones, lines 1527–1528); First Aid 2025 GI (liver tissue architecture, fa_gastro_cache lines 594–595). The lecture describes zone 2 as intermediate in oxygen and substrates (SDL 12, section 2, lines 2321–2322).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 2 has intermediate oxygen tension and metabolic activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 3 has the highest concentration of cytochrome P450 (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — hepatic injury, scarring, repair, and liver failure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a liver biopsy examined by electron microscopy. The sinusoidal endothelium shows large pores without diaphragms and wide gaps between cells. Which capillary type is this?","choices":{"A":"Continuous type","B":"Diaphragmed fenestrated","C":"Blood brain barrier type","D":"Glomerular type","E":"Discontinuous type"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatic sinusoids have discontinuous endothelium with large fenestrae up to 1 µm that lack diaphragms, wide intercellular gaps, and no continuous basal lamina. Continuous and blood brain barrier capillaries have tight junctions and no pores. Diaphragmed fenestrated capillaries line gut mucosa and endocrine glands. Glomerular capillaries have fenestrae on a thick basement membrane. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium, lines 2345–2349).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excess Disse fluid drains to the periportal spaces and forms hepatic lymph (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: discontinuous sinusoids allow plasma proteins to reach hepatocytes without obstruction (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has a low dietary intake of vitamin A for months. When his blood level falls, the liver releases stored vitamin A into the blood bound to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Ceruloplasmin","B":"Thyroxine binding globulin","C":"Retinol binding protein","D":"Vitamin D binding protein","E":"Cortisol binding globulin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When systemic vitamin A drops, stellate cells release retinol bound to retinol binding protein into the blood. Ceruloplasmin carries copper, and the binding globulins and vitamin D binding protein carry thyroxine, cortisol, and vitamin D. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, vitamin A storage, lines 2384–2388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells hold vitamin A in cytoplasmic lipid droplets (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: stellate cells lose their lipid droplets when they become myofibroblasts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile salt micelles needed to absorb fat-soluble vitamins such as A and D","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":3,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman studies liver histology with her tutor, who asks how the walls of a bile canaliculus are formed. What forms them?","choices":{"A":"Apposed hepatocyte grooves","B":"Cholangiocyte lined tubes","C":"Endothelial lined channels","D":"Kupffer cell processes","E":"Stellate cell processes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are intercellular channels formed by apposed grooves in the membranes of adjacent hepatocytes and sealed by tight junctions. Cholangiocytes line ductules and ducts, endothelium lines sinusoids, and Kupffer and stellate cells do not form canaliculi. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile canaliculi empty into canals of Hering at the lobule margin (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: the right and left hepatic ducts form the common hepatic duct (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has a needle liver biopsy, and the pathologist measures one classic hexagonal lobule. About what size is a classic lobule across?","choices":{"A":"10 to 20 µm","B":"1 to 2 mm","C":"50 to 100 µm","D":"1 to 2 cm","E":"5 to 10 cm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The classic hepatic lobule is a roughly hexagonal prism about 1 to 2 mm across, centered on a central vein with portal tracts at its six angles. The micrometer sizes are cell to tissue scale, far below a lobule, since a hepatocyte is only about 20 to 30 µm, and the centimeter sizes match whole lobes or masses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, classic hepatic lobule structure, lines 2287–2290).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the classic lobule centers on a terminal hepatic venule (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: First Aid describes the lobule as a central vein with portal triads on its edges (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true}],"13":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what type of epithelium lines the gallbladder mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium","D":"Simple columnar epithelium","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The gallbladder is lined by a single layer of tall columnar absorptive cells with short apical microvilli, apical tight junctions, and abundant mitochondria. Cuboidal, stratified squamous, pseudostratified ciliated, and transitional linings are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, simple columnar epithelium, lines 2428–2431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have apical tight junctions that seal the lumen (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the gallbladder has a fundus, body, infundibulum, and neck (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which two layers are missing from the gallbladder wall?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa and serosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae and submucosa","C":"Lamina propria and adventitia","D":"Epithelium and lamina propria","E":"Adventitia and muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gallbladder lacks both a muscularis mucosae and a submucosa, so the lamina propria rests directly on the muscularis externa. It has an epithelium, a lamina propria, a muscularis externa, and an adventitia on the liver side with serosa on the free surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, absence of muscularis mucosae and submucosa, lines 2438–2440).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lamina propria of the gallbladder rests directly upon the muscularis externa (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the free surface of the gallbladder is covered by visceral peritoneum (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, what does the gallbladder lamina propria noticeably lack?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic vessels","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Plasma cells","D":"Resident lymphocytes","E":"Small venules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The gallbladder lamina propria is cellular loose connective tissue rich in fenestrated capillaries, small venules, lymphocytes, and plasma cells, but it noticeably lacks lymphatic vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, lamina propria, lines 2435–2437).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the empty gallbladder mucosa forms deep temporary folds that flatten as the organ fills (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: calculous cholecystitis follows gallstone impaction in the cystic duct and thickens the gallbladder wall (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which pump on the lateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells drives active electrolyte transport?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen potassium ATPase","B":"Calcium ATPase pumps","C":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Sodium potassium ATPase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral membrane Na+/K+ ATPase pumps drive transport of Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular space. The hydrogen potassium ATPase is the gastric proton pump. Calcium pumps, sodium glucose cotransport, and a chloride bicarbonate exchanger are not named as the driver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, active electrolyte transport, lines 2456–2459).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder cells move Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular compartment (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: solute build up in the narrow lateral space sets up an osmotic gradient with the lumen (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which membrane water channels carry water across gallbladder epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Aquaporins 1 and 8","B":"Aquaporins 2 and 3","C":"Aquaporins 4 and 5","D":"Aquaporins 0 and 7","E":"Connexin channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Water crosses the apical and basolateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells through aquaporin 1 and aquaporin 8. The other aquaporin pairs are not described for the gallbladder, and connexins form gap junctions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, aquaporin mediated water absorption, lines 2464–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water movement in the gallbladder is passive and follows the osmotic gradient (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: bile is stored within the gallbladder between meals (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary physiology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the water in incoming hepatic bile does the gallbladder remove?","choices":{"A":"About 50 percent","B":"About 90 percent","C":"About 25 percent","D":"About 10 percent","E":"About 70 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Coupled electrolyte and water reabsorption removes about 90 percent of the water from hepatic bile. The lower figures understate the concentrating power of the gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, net bile concentration, lines 2472–2474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder concentration raises bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin together (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: gallbladder stasis promotes sludge and gallstone formation (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, how is the exocrine pancreas classified as a gland?","choices":{"A":"Compound tubuloacinar serous gland","B":"Simple branched tubular mucous gland","C":"Compound tubular mixed mucous gland","D":"Simple coiled tubular gland","E":"Compound alveolar seromucous gland"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The exocrine pancreas is a compound tubuloacinar serous gland divided into ill defined lobules by thin septa from its capsule. Simple branched, simple coiled, mucous, and seromucous patterns describe other glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, overall organization, lines 2478–2479).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the exocrine pancreas makes up the majority of pancreatic mass (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: thin septa from the capsule divide the pancreas into ill defined lobules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist must decide whether a section of serous gland came from the pancreas or the parotid. Which feature found within the acini identifies the pancreas?","choices":{"A":"Basophilic basal cytoplasm","B":"Apical zymogen granules","C":"Pale centroacinar cells","D":"Serous demilunes on mucous cells","E":"Pyramidal secretory cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pale, flattened centroacinar cells at the center of the acinus are the intra-acinar start of the intercalated ducts, a feature unique to the pancreas. Basophilic basal cytoplasm, apical zymogen granules, and pyramidal secretory cells are shared by serous acini such as those of the parotid. Serous demilunes capping mucous acini belong to mixed glands such as the submandibular gland. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells are a unique feature of the pancreas, p71); Pawlina (serous demilunes of the submandibular gland).","boardPrep":"Step 1: centroacinar cells lack zymogen granules and secrete an isotonic sodium bicarbonate fluid (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: secretin acts on duct and centroacinar cells through cAMP (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — duct and centroacinar bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, pale centroacinar cells mark the intra-acinar start of which ducts?","choices":{"A":"Interlobular ducts","B":"Striated ducts","C":"Main pancreatic duct","D":"Intercalated ducts","E":"Intralobular ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Centroacinar cells are the intra-acinar origin of the intercalated ducts, a feature unique to the pancreas. Intralobular and interlobular ducts lie farther downstream, striated ducts belong to salivary glands, and the main duct is the final collecting channel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2491).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic secretions pass from acini to centroacinar cells, intercalated ducts, and pancreatic ducts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: centroacinar cells lack zymogen granules and ergastoplasm and stain faintly (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which cells release secretin when acid enters the small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal I cells","B":"Duodenal S cells","C":"Jejunal K cells","D":"Gastric G cells","E":"Ileal L cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when gastric acid lowers duodenal pH below about 4.5. I cells release CCK, K cells release GIP, G cells release gastrin, and L cells release GLP 1 and PYY. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin source and trigger, lines 2507–2508).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and bile secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: secretin also decreases parietal cell H+ secretion and gastric motility (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, CCK1 receptors on the pancreatic target cells of CCK activate which signaling pathway?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"IP3 and calcium","D":"JAK STAT signaling","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and activates the IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ pathway, causing zymogen granule exocytosis. Cyclic AMP is the secretin pathway in ductal cells. Cyclic GMP, JAK STAT, and tyrosine kinase signaling are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, CCK target cell and pathway, lines 2519–2520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: CCK also slows gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which phase accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Cephalic phase","B":"Gastric phase","C":"Interdigestive phase","D":"Oral phase","E":"Intestinal phase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin, CCK, and vagal ACh act together during the intestinal phase, which accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion. The cephalic and gastric phases contribute less, and the interdigestive and oral phases are not the main period. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, coordinated action, lines 2524–2527).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin and CCK act synergistically with vagal ACh to deliver an alkaline enzyme rich juice (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits the exocrine pancreas (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, about what share of the pancreas is endocrine tissue?","choices":{"A":"80 to 85 percent","B":"10 to 15 percent","C":"1 to 2 percent","D":"25 to 30 percent","E":"5 to 8 percent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The endocrine pancreas makes up only 1 to 2 percent of the organ as scattered islets, while the exocrine pancreas makes up 80 to 85 percent. The other figures are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, histologic differentiation, lines 2533–2536).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic endocrine function is glucose regulation by insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreas physiology). COMLEX: islets are most concentrated in the tail of the pancreas (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, where in a human islet are beta cells mainly located?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Capsule surface","C":"Acinar lumen","D":"Central core","E":"Duct walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta cells, about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells, lie mainly in the central core of the islet. Alpha and delta cells lie mainly at the periphery. Capsules, acinar lumens, and duct walls are not islet locations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2541).","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make up about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells and produce insulin (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: islet cells form irregular cords invested by fenestrated capillaries (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"According to the lecture, which electron microscopic feature marks the 325 nm granules of islet delta cells?","choices":{"A":"Crystalline polyhedral core","B":"Round eccentric core","C":"Clear peripheral halo","D":"Lamellar body","E":"Homogeneous matrix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delta cell granules are about 325 nm with a homogeneous matrix. Beta cell granules have a crystalline polyhedral core with a clear halo, and alpha cell granules have a dense round eccentric core. Lamellar bodies are not islet granules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta cells make up about 5 to 10 percent of islet cells and sit at the islet periphery (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: PP cells make pancreatic polypeptide, which inhibits intestinal motility and pancreatic secretion (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with gallstones and chronic cholecystitis has a cholecystectomy. Histology shows deep mucosal outpouchings that extend through the lamina propria into the muscularis externa. What are these structures?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal crypts of Lieberkuhn","B":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","C":"Submucosal Brunner glands","D":"Periportal canals of Hering","E":"Pancreatic acinar rests"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are deep mucosal invaginations that extend into or through the muscularis externa. They arise from epithelial hyperplasia and herniation under luminal pressure, and trapped bile and bacteria promote chronic inflammation and stones. Crypts of Lieberkuhn and Brunner glands belong to the intestine, canals of Hering belong to the liver, and acinar rests are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, lines 2448–2452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile stagnation in mucosal outpouchings promotes chronic gallbladder inflammation and gallstones (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: a porcelain gallbladder is a calcified wall from chronic cholecystitis (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 4 — chronic cholecystitis and porcelain gallbladder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and the surgeon dissects the gallbladder off the liver bed. Which layer covers the surface that was attached to the liver?","choices":{"A":"Visceral serosa","B":"Submucosal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Fibrous adventitia","E":"Parietal peritoneum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The attached surface is covered by a connective tissue adventitia carrying large vessels, lymphatics, and nerves, while the free surface has a smooth serosa of visceral peritoneum. The gallbladder lacks a submucosa and a muscularis mucosae, and parietal peritoneum lines the body wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, adventitia versus serosa, lines 2444–2447).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the adventitia on the liver side carries large blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: acalculous cholecystitis is due to stasis, hypoperfusion, or infection in critically ill patients (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his gallbladder squeezes bile into the cystic duct. Which wall component produces this contraction?","choices":{"A":"Interlacing smooth muscle","B":"Circular skeletal muscle","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Myoepithelial cells","E":"Longitudinal skeletal muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscularis externa is made of randomly oriented interlacing smooth muscle bundles with elastic and collagen fibers, and its contraction reduces luminal volume to expel bile. The gallbladder has no skeletal muscle or muscularis mucosae, and myoepithelial cells belong to glands such as salivary glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, muscularis externa, lines 2441–2443).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after a fatty meal, CCK triggers gallbladder contraction that can force a stone into the cystic duct (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology). COMLEX: biliary colic worsens after meals because CCK drives gallbladder contraction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary colic).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her gallbladder changes the bile it stores. By about how much are bile salts concentrated during storage?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"100 to 200 times","C":"4 to 5 times","D":"50 to 60 times","E":"10 to 20 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Removal of about 90 percent of the water concentrates bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin up to 10 to 20 times. The smaller figures understate, and the larger figures overstate, the effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, net bile concentration, lines 2472–2474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: most gallstones form within the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: raised cholesterol, reduced bile salts, and gallbladder stasis all cause sludge or stones (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — formation and composition of gallstones","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has a cholecystectomy, and the pathologist explains how the gallbladder absorbs fluid. After isotonic fluid crosses the basal lamina into the lamina propria, where is it taken up?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic capillaries","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Lumen of the cystic duct","D":"Bile in the gallbladder lumen","E":"Muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hydrostatic pressure in the lateral spaces pushes isotonic fluid across the basal lamina, where fenestrated capillaries rapidly absorb it. The lamina propria noticeably lacks lymphatics, fluid does not return to the lumen or cystic duct, and the muscularis does not absorb it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, hydrostatic fluid movement, lines 2468–2471).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water build up distends the lateral intercellular space and raises local hydrostatic pressure (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the lamina propria of the gallbladder has fenestrated capillaries and small venules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and her surgeon explains how a living gallbladder concentrates bile. Water moves from the lumen through membrane water channels into the lateral intercellular space. What drives this water movement?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping","B":"Bile salt uptake","C":"Osmotic gradient","D":"Vesicular transport","E":"Ciliary beating"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Actively pumped electrolytes collect in the lateral space and create an osmotic gradient, and water follows passively through aquaporins. Water is not pumped actively, bile salts are concentrated rather than absorbed, and vesicles and cilia are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, osmotic gradient and water absorption, lines 2460–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have abundant apical and basal mitochondria for active transport (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the striated border of gallbladder cells is formed by short apical microvilli (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has recurrent pneumonia, poor weight gain, greasy stools, and a raised sweat chloride. Which apical protein of his pancreatic duct cells is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"ENaC sodium channel","B":"Aquaporin water channel","C":"Voltage gated K channel","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"CFTR chloride channel"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ductal cells secrete Na+ and HCO3- rich fluid through CFTR Cl- channels working with apical Cl-/HCO3- exchangers. A defective CFTR reduces this watery output. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger is the partner of CFTR but is not the defective protein in cystic fibrosis. ENaC, aquaporins, and potassium channels are not the described apical secretory step. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, ductal cell secretory function, lines 2500–2503).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ductal secretion brings duodenal pH to about 8.0 for pancreatic enzyme activity (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: pancreatic fluid is isotonic with water, bicarbonate, and other electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a pancreatic biopsy. In H&E sections, pale flattened cells sit in the center of each acinus and lack zymogen granules. What do these cells mainly secrete?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bicarbonate fluid","B":"Trypsinogen and proelastase","C":"Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide","D":"Mucin and lysozyme","E":"Pancreatic amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Centroacinar cells secrete the initial isotonic aqueous fluid rich in sodium and bicarbonate. Acinar cells secrete proenzymes and amylase, islet cells secrete insulin and pancreatic polypeptide, and mucin and lysozyme are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2493).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intercalated ducts secrete bicarbonate (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: at high postprandial flow, the bicarbonate to chloride ratio of pancreatic fluid rises (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has acute pancreatitis with raised serum enzymes. Which pancreatic enzymes do acinar cells secrete in active form rather than as proenzymes?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and elastase","B":"Chymotrypsin and trypsin","C":"Carboxypeptidase and elastase","D":"Amylase and lipase","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete proteases as proenzymes such as trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and proelastase, but release pancreatic amylase and lipases as active enzymes. Enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than an acinar product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, acinar cell secretory function, lines 2484–2486).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic fluid contains zymogens such as trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: pancreatic proteases are secreted as proenzymes also called zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man eats a meal, and acidic chyme drops his duodenal pH below 4.5. Which pancreatic secretion does the resulting hormone mainly stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Low volume enzyme rich juice","B":"Mucus rich interlobular secretion","C":"Insulin rich islet output","D":"Chloride rich low flow fluid","E":"High volume bicarbonate fluid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acid releases secretin from S cells, which acts on ductal and centroacinar cells to produce a copious watery fluid rich in sodium bicarbonate with little enzyme. Low volume enzyme rich juice is the CCK response, and chloride rich fluid is the low flow basal pattern. Interlobular goblet cells add little mucus, and islet insulin is not an exocrine output of secretin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin, lines 2506–2514).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released by acid and fatty acids in the duodenal lumen (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones). COMLEX: S cells that release secretin are found in the duodenum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman eats a meal rich in fat and protein, and fatty acids and phenylalanine stimulate duodenal I cells. Which pancreatic cells are the main target of the released hormone?","choices":{"A":"Ductal cells","B":"Centroacinar cells","C":"Acinar cells","D":"Beta cells","E":"Stellate cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"I cells release CCK, which binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and triggers zymogen granule exocytosis for a low volume enzyme rich secretion. Ductal and centroacinar cells answer mainly to secretin, beta cells are endocrine, and stellate cells are not the target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, cholecystokinin, lines 2515–2523).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK stimulates zymogen release from acinar cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: First Aid places CCK producing I cells in the duodenum and jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with suspected gastrinoma has a secretin stimulation test. In the pancreas, secretin binds ductal cell receptors that raise which intracellular messenger?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Cyclic AMP","E":"Diacylglycerol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretin binds receptors on ductal and centroacinar cells and stimulates adenylyl cyclase to raise intracellular cAMP. IP3 and calcium are the CCK pathway in acinar cells. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and diacylglycerol are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin target cell and pathway, lines 2509–2511).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison workup uses fasting gastrin and gastric pH, then a secretin stimulation test (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma). COMLEX: secretin stimulates bicarbonate release from ductal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has new type 1 diabetes from autoimmune loss of insulin producing islet cells. On electron microscopy, which granule appearance marked the destroyed cells?","choices":{"A":"Round eccentric core","B":"Polyhedral core with halo","C":"Homogeneous granule matrix","D":"Large zymogen type granule","E":"Lamellated concentric layers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta cell granules are about 300 nm with a dense crystalline polyhedral core surrounded by a clear halo. A round eccentric core marks alpha cells, a homogeneous matrix marks delta cells, large zymogen granules belong to acinar cells, and lamellated layers are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2543).","boardPrep":"Step 1: islets are pale staining, highly vascular clusters scattered among dark serous acini (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the pancreas holds about 1 to 3 million islets of Langerhans (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, the beta cell tumor, contrasted with beta cell loss in type 1 diabetes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has a pancreatic islet tumor that secretes excess glucagon. In a normal human islet, where do the cells that make this hormone mainly sit?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Central islet core","C":"Duct epithelium","D":"Acinar lumen","E":"Capsular septa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha cells, about 15 to 20 percent of islet cells, sit primarily at the islet periphery in humans and make glucagon, which stimulates glycogenolysis and raises blood glucose. Beta cells occupy the central core. Ducts, acinar lumens, and septa are not islet sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, alpha cells, lines 2544–2547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha cell granules are about 250 nm with a dense round eccentric core (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: glucagon raises blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with a delta cell tumor of the pancreas has new hyperglycemia and fatty stools. Inhibition of which outputs by the excess hormone explains both findings?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon and bicarbonate output","B":"Amylin and mucin secretion","C":"Insulin and enzyme secretion","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide output","E":"Gastrin and secretin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Delta cells make somatostatin, which suppresses insulin and glucagon release as well as exocrine secretion. Less insulin raises glucose, and less enzyme output causes fatty stools. Less glucagon would lower rather than raise glucose, and amylin, mucin, pancreatic polypeptide, gastrin, and secretin do not explain both findings. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatinomas arise from delta cells with autonomous somatostatin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatinoma). COMLEX: somatostatin decreases insulin, glucagon, CCK, and secretin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a fasting ultrasound, and her empty gallbladder mucosa shows many deep folds. What happens to these folds as the gallbladder fills with bile?","choices":{"A":"They flatten out","B":"They deepen further","C":"They form villi","D":"They calcify","E":"They become polyps"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the empty or partly filled state the gallbladder mucosa forms many deep temporary folds, which flatten out as the organ distends with bile. The folds do not deepen, form villi, calcify, or become polyps. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, mucosal folds, lines 2432–2434).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gallbladder lies in the right upper quadrant beneath the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy). COMLEX: the gallbladder gets sensory innervation from the phrenic nerve (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder storage and concentration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has gallbladder bile that is far more concentrated than his hepatic bile. Which solutes do his gallbladder epithelial cells actively pump into the lateral intercellular spaces?","choices":{"A":"Potassium and hydrogen ions","B":"Calcium and magnesium ions","C":"Conjugated bile salts and bilirubin","D":"Glucose and free amino acids","E":"Sodium chloride and bicarbonate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gallbladder epithelial cells actively transport Na+, Cl- and HCO3- across their lateral membranes into the intercellular space, and water follows. Potassium and hydrogen, calcium and magnesium, and nutrients are not the described solutes, and bile salts and bilirubin stay in the lumen and become concentrated. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, active electrolyte transport, lines 2456–2459).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct to form the common bile duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy). COMLEX: the gallbladder is supplied by the cystic artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has a biopsy of a large interlobular pancreatic duct. Besides simple columnar lining cells, which occasional cells are expected in its epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Paneth and intestinal tuft cells","B":"Parietal and gastric chief cells","C":"Goblet and enteroendocrine cells","D":"Ciliated and respiratory basal cells","E":"Hepatic Kupffer and stellate cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Larger interlobular ducts are lined by simple columnar epithelium with occasional enteroendocrine and goblet cells, while intercalated ducts have low cuboidal cells continuous with centroacinar cells. Paneth, parietal, chief, ciliated, Kupffer, and stellate cells belong to other organs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, ductal cells, lines 2494–2499).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the main pancreatic duct empties into the major duodenal papilla (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology figure, fa_gastro_cache lines 198–204; also Bootcamp merged_with_ocr line 2961). COMLEX: the accessory pancreatic duct opens at the minor papilla (First Aid 2025 GI pancreas embryology figure, fa_gastro_cache lines 198–204; also Bootcamp merged_with_ocr line 2962).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man drinks a protein shake, and duodenal I cells release their hormone in response to specific amino acids. Besides phenylalanine and methionine, which amino acid is an especially strong trigger?","choices":{"A":"Glycine","B":"Tryptophan","C":"Glutamine","D":"Asparagine","E":"Hydroxyproline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"I cells release CCK in response to fatty acids, monoglycerides, peptides, and amino acids, especially phenylalanine, methionine, and tryptophan. Glycine, glutamine, asparagine, and hydroxyproline are not named. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, CCK source and trigger, lines 2516–2518).","boardPrep":"Step 1: I cells release CCK in the duodenum and jejunum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells). COMLEX: CCK is released in response to fatty acids and amino acids (First Aid 2025 GI hormones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman dies of heart failure, and at autopsy the pathologist counts pancreatic islets region by region. In which region are islets most concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic head","B":"Pancreatic neck","C":"Uncinate process","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Pancreatic body"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Islets of Langerhans are scattered among the exocrine acini and are most concentrated in the tail. The head, neck, uncinate process, and body hold fewer islets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, histologic differentiation, lines 2535–2538).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic veins drain into the portal circulation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic vasculature, merged_with_ocr line 2966). COMLEX: the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery give rise to the pancreaticoduodenal arteries (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic vasculature, merged_with_ocr line 2965).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has a biopsy of the pancreas. A rare islet cell type makes up a small minority of islet cells and is also scattered in the exocrine tissue. Its hormone slows intestinal motility. Which hormone is it?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic polypeptide","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Gastric inhibitory peptide","D":"Glucagon like peptide 1","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PP or F cells make up 1 to 2 percent of islet cells, are also scattered in the exocrine pancreas, and make pancreatic polypeptide, which inhibits intestinal motility and pancreatic enzyme and bicarbonate secretion. VIP, GIP, and GLP 1 come from other cells, and somatostatin comes from delta cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, PP cells, lines 2552–2554).","boardPrep":"Step 1: L cells in the ileum release GLP 1, GLP 2, and PYY (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells). COMLEX: K cells in the duodenum and jejunum release GIP (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true}]};
